nimal possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nSince each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, there exists a permutation  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nDenote  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Decomposition of the board  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\na_i=\\sigma(i)-1,\\qquad b_i=n-\\sigma(i).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a_i\\) (resp. \\(b_i\\)) is the number of unit squares to the left (resp. right) of the uncovered cell in row \\(i\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le a_i\\},\n\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;a_i+2\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe three sets \\(L,R,U\\) are pairwise disjoint and  \n\n\\[\nL\\cup R\\cup U=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\{a_i\\}\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i=\\frac{n(n-1)}2,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}b_i=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound of \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nIn the set \\(L\\) the cells  \n\n\\[\np_i=(i,a_i)\\qquad\\text{for those }i\\text{ with }a_i\\ge1\n\\]\n\nlie in distinct columns, and any axis‑parallel rectangle contained in \\(L\\) can contain at most one of them.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a rectangle \\(T\\) contains two distinct cells \\(p_i\\) and \\(p_{i'}\\) with \\(a_i<a_{i'}\\), then the column interval of \\(T\\) contains column \\(a_{i'}\\).  \nConsequently row \\(i\\) would contain a cell of \\(T\\) in column \\(a_{i'}\\), but row \\(i\\) has no 