 at least  \n\n\\[\n1+(n-2)=n-1\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\nFormally, we prove by induction on \\(k\\) that the upper\u2011triangular region of a\n\\(k\\times k\\) board requires at least \\(k-1\\) rectangles.  The base case\n\\(k=1\\) is trivial (the region is empty).  The inductive step is exactly the\nargument above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound for the lower\u2011triangular region\n\nThe lower\u2011triangular region \\(L\\) is the transpose of \\(U\\); the same induction\napplies, giving the same lower bound \\(n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Global lower bound\n\nSince a tile cannot intersect both \\(U\\) and \\(L\\), the total number of tiles in\nany admissible tiling satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction achieving the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(f(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main\ndiagonal \\((i,i)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n*Horizontal tiles.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_i\\) is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\); it does not contain the hole\n\\((i,i)\\) and lies completely inside the upper\u2011triangular region.\n\n*Vertical tiles.*  \nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_j\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times 1\\); it avoids the hole \\((j,j)\\) and\nlies completely inside the lower\u2011triangular region.\n\n*Disjointness.*  \nTwo distinct horizontal tiles occupy different rows, so they are disjoint.\nTwo distinct vertical tiles occupy different columns, so they are disjoint.\nA horizontal tile \\(H_i\\) contains cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>i\\); a vertical tile\n\\(V_j\\) contains cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\).  The inequalities \\(j>i\\) and\n\\(i>j\\) cannot hold simultaneously, hence \\(H_i\\cap V_j=\\varnothing\\) for all\n\\(i,j\\).  Consequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{H_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{V_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nis a partition of \\(C\\) into \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved that any admissible tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles and\nhave exhibited a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  Therefore the minimum\npossible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
