y cell of row n because (n,n) is a hole. But they say \"the only cell belonging to column n is (1,n)\". That is incorrect. Let's examine the logic: They want to argue that any rectangle covering (1,n) cannot contain any cell of row n because (n,n) is uncovered. But the rectangle covering (1,n) could have height >1, but then it would include (k,n) for some k>1. Since (k,n) is in column n, but row k < n? Actually (k,n) with k>1 is also in column n, but not diagonal. However, if rectangle includes (k,n) for k>1, then rectangle includes row k and column n. Since row k includes the diagonal cell (k,k) which is uncovered, but rectangle includes column n, not necessarily column k. Wait, if rectangle includes rows I = [a,b] and columns J = [c,d]. If rectangle includes (1,n), then 1 ∈ I, n ∈ J. Since J is an interval containing n, J must be of the form [c,n] where c ≤ n. Since rectangle includes (1,n), I includes 1, so I = [1,b] perhaps. Now rectangle includes any cell (i,j) with i∈I, j∈J. If b≥n? Actually b ≤ n because rows go up to n. So rectangle includes all cells (i,j) where i ∈ [1,b] and j ∈ [c,n]. For any i in I and j in J. If b≥c (i.e., intervals intersect), then rectangle includes diagonal cells (k,k) for k ∈ I∩J. Since diagonal cells are uncovered, rectangle cannot intersect diagonal. So to avoid diagonal, we must have I∩J = ∅. That means either b < c (the max row index is less than min column index) or c > b. Since rectangle includes (1,n), we have I includes 1, J includes n. So we require I∩J = ∅. Since J includes n, and I includes 1, we need either max(I) < min(J) or min(I) > max(J). Since min(I)=1, max(J)=n, the second case cannot hold (1 > n false). So we need max(I) < min(J). That means the highest row index in I is less than the smallest column index in J. Since J includes n, min(J) ≤ n. Actually J includes n, but J is an interval [c,n] where c ≤ n. So min(J) = c. So we need max(I) < c. Since I includes 1, max(I) = b. So we need b < c. That means the rectangle's rows are from 1 to b, columns from c to n, with b < c. So the rectangle is strictly above the diagonal (i<j). So any rectangle covering (1,n) must have rows only up to some b < c ≤ n, and columns from c to n. Since (1,n) is the top right corner of the rectangle, the rectangle must be of height 1? Let's examine: For (1,n) to be in rectangle, we need 1 ∈ I and n ∈ J. Since I = [a,b] includes 1, a ≤ 1 ≤ b. Since rows are positive, a=1. So I = [1,b] for some b≥1. J = [c,n] for some c ≤ n. The condition b < c ensures no diagonal cells. Since b < c ≤ n, we have b ≤ n-1. So the rectangle's rows are 1..b, columns are c..n, with b < c. So the rectangle's height is b (since a=1). So height could be >1 if b>1. So rectangle covering (1,n) could have height >1, as long as b < c. For example, suppose b=2, c=4, n=5. Then rectangle includes rows 1-2, columns 4-5. It includes (1,5) and (2,5). That's allowed because (2,5) is also i<j. So the rectangle covering (1,n) does not necessarily have height 1. The solution's claim that \"its height is 1 and it must be the whole first row { (1,j) | 2 ≤ j ≤ n }\" is not correct. Let's examine more carefully: They claim \"the only cell belonging to column n is (1,n)\". That's false. Actually there are many cells in column n. So the rectangle covering (1,n) could also include (2,n), (3,n), etc., as long as those rows are ≤ b < c. But c is the leftmost column of rectangle, and must be > b. So if rectangle includes column n, the leftmost column c must be > b. So if b = 2, c must be at least 3. So rectangle includes columns 3..n, rows 1..2. So it includes (2,n) as well. So the rectangle covering (1,n) could be larger than just the first row.\n\nThus the induction step is flawed. However, they might be using a different argument: They say \"the only cell belonging to column n is (1,n)\". That's false. So the induction step is invalid. The lower bound may be larger than n-1? Let's think about the minimal number of rectangles needed to tile the up