a(I) for I = [1,3] = {1,2,3} maps to {sigma(1), sigma(2), sigma(3)} = {2,5,7}, which is not an interval. So the image of an interval under a permutation is not necessarily an interval, so the shape may not be a rectangle (i.e., product of intervals). So the claim that the family of rectangles remains a partition of the covered cells after applying a row permutation is false: the rectangles may become non-rectangular shapes (disconnected rows). However, they claim that the number of tiles is unchanged. But the shape of the tiles may not be rectangles after permutation, which is not allowed. However, they are using the permutation to argue that the minimal number of tiles is the same for any permutation of uncovered squares, because we can relabel rows (i.e., rename rows) without changing the problem. Actually, we can rename rows and columns (i.e., reorder them) without affecting the tiling problem because the grid is symmetric: we can permute rows and columns simultaneously? Wait, they only permute rows, not columns. The problem is symmetric under row permutations and column permutations, but the tiling must be on the original grid. However, we can think of applying a permutation to the labeling of rows (i.e., re-indexing rows) is just a renaming, not a physical transformation. The number of tiles needed is independent of labeling. So the argument is that we can assume uncovered squares are on diagonal by re-indexing rows (or columns). This is valid: we can rename rows (i.e., apply a bijection on row indices) and rename columns accordingly? Actually, we need to preserve the condition that each row and column has exactly one uncovered square. If we rename rows via sigma, the uncovered squares become (sigma(i), f(i)). But we also rename columns? They only rename rows, not columns. Let's examine: Initially uncovered squares are at (i, f(i)). They define sigma(i) = f(i). Then they \"re-label row i as row f(i)\". That is, they permute rows according to sigma. So after rel