n\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n     \\[\n     L_j=\\{(i,j):i>j\\},\n     \\]  \n     which covers all cells left of the diagonal in column \\(j\\).  \n   - For each \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n     \\[\n     R_j=\\{(i,j):i<j\\},\n     \\]  \n     which covers all cells right of the diagonal in column \\(j\\).  \n\n   The rectangles \\(\\{L_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{R_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint, together they cover every cell except the diagonal holes, and there are exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) of them.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\nA placement of tiles must satisfy that each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is a **permutation matrix**: there exists a permutation  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j<p(i)\\},\n\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j>p(i)\\}\n\\]\n\nthe sets of unit squares lying respectively **left** and **right** of the hole in each row.  \nClearly  \n\n\\[\nL\\cup R=\\{(i,j)\\mid (i,j)\\neq (i,p(i))\\},\n\\qquad \nL\\cap R=\\varnothing .\n\\]\n\nA tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose interior consists only of squares from \\(L\\cup R\\).  \nThus a tiling of the board is exactly a partition of \\(L\\cup R\\) into such rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\ni_j:=p^{-1}(j+1),\n\\]\n\nthe unique row whose hole lies in column \\(j+1\\).  \nThe cell \\((i_j,j)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) because \\(j<p(i_j)=j+1\\).\n\nConsider any rectangle \\(Q\\) that contains \\((i_j,j)\\).  \nIf \\(Q\\) also contained column \\(j+1\\), then it would contain the hole \\((i_j,j+1)\\), which is forbidden.  \nSince the column interval of a rectangle is a contiguous set of columns, the presence of column \\(j+1\\) would force the inclusion of the hole.  \nHence the column interval of \\(Q\\) is a subset of \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,j\\}\\); in particular \\(Q\\) does **not** contain any column larger than \\(j\\).\n\nConsequently a rectangle that covers \\((i_j,j)\\) cannot cover any cell \\((i_{j'},j')\\) with \\(j'\\neq j\\), because such a cell lies in a column \\(j'>j\\) (or \\(j'<j\\) and the rectangle would have to contain column \\(j\\!+\\!1\\)).  \nThus the \\(n-1\\) distinct cells  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i_j,j)\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nrequire at least \\(n-1\\) different rectangles.  \nHence any tiling uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles to cover the left region \\(L\\).\n\nAn analogous argument applied to the right region \\(R\\) yields a second lower bound.  \nFor each \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nk_j:=p^{-1}(j-1),\n\\]\n\nthe row whose hole lies in column \\(j-1\\).  \nThe cell \\((k_j,j)\\) lies in \\(R\\) because \\(j>p(k_j)=j-1\\).  \nAny rectangle covering \\((k_j,j)\\) cannot contain column \\(j-1\\); therefore its column interval is contained in \\(\\{j,j+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and it cannot contain any cell \\((k_{j'},j')\\) with \\(j'\\neq j\\).  \nThus the \\(n-1\\) cells \\(\\{(k_j,j)\\mid j=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) force at least another \\(n-1\\) distinct rectangles.\n\nSummarising, every admissible tiling needs at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An upper bound – a construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the **identity permutation** \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\nDefine the following rectangles:\n\n*For the left side* (cells with column index smaller than the row index):\n\\[\nL_j:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\},\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n\\]\nEach \\(L_j\\) is a vertical strip of width one column, extending from row \\(j+1\\) down to row \\(n\\).\n\n*For the right side* (cells with column index larger than the row index):\n\\[\nR_j:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\},\\qquad j=2,3,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\nEach \\(R_j\\) is a vertical strip of width one column, extending from row \\(1\\) up to row \\(j-1\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{L_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{R_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint, because a cell \\((i,j)\\) satisfies either \\(i>j\\) (hence belongs to exactly one \\(L_j\\)) or \\(i<j\\) (hence belongs to exactly one \\(R_j\\)), never both.  \nEvery non‑diagonal cell belongs to one of these rectangles, and the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) are left uncovered, as required.\n\nThe total number of rectangles used is  \n\n\\[\n| \\{L_j\\}|+| \\{R_j\\}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nThe lower bound proved in §1 shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nThe construction of §2 shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe smallest number of rectangular tiles that Matilda can place while leaving exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column of a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
