ble number of tiles is \\(2(n-1)=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nIn each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) exactly one unit square is left uncovered; denote its column by \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation.\n\nDefine the two disjoint sets of covered squares  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;\\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery tile is an axis\u2011parallel rectangle that contains no uncovered square, hence each tile lies completely inside \\(L\\) or completely inside \\(R\\).  \nConsequently the total number of tiles equals  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}= \\#\\text{tiles covering }L+\\#\\text{tiles covering }R .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for the left region \\(L\\)\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\ni_k:=\\text{the unique index with }\\sigma(i_k)=k .\n\\]\n\nThe square  \n\n\\[\ns_k:=(i_k,k-1)\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nbelongs to \\(L\\) because \\(k-1<\\sigma(i_k)=k\\).\n\nConsider any rectangle \\(T\\) that covers \\(s_k\\).  \nSince column \\(k\\) is the uncovered square in row \\(i_k\\), the column interval of \\(T\\) must be a subset of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\); otherwise \\(T\\) would contain the uncovered square \\((i_k,k)\\).  \nBecause \\(s_k\\) lies in column \\(k-1\\), the rightmost column of \\(T\\) is exactly \\(k-1\\).\n\nNow suppose a single rectangle covered two distinct squares \\(s_k\\) and \\(s_{k'}\\) with \\(k<k'\\).  \nIts column interval would have to contain both columns \\(k-1\\) and \\(k'-1\\), so its rightmost column would be at least \\(k'-1\\ge k\\).  \nBut then the rectangle would also contain column \\(k\\) in row \\(i_k\\), contradicting the fact that column \\(k\\) is uncovered in that row.  \nHence **no rectangle can contain two different squares \\(s_k\\)**.\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) squares of the form \\(s_k\\) (for \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\)), and each rectangle can cover at most one of them.  \nTherefore any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles:\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles covering }L\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the right region \\(R\\)\n\nThe argument for \\(R\\) is symmetric.  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nj_k:=\\text{the unique index with }\\sigma(j_k)=k .\n\\]\n\nThe square  \n\n\\[\nt_k:=(j_k,k+1)\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nlies in \\(R\\) because \\(k+1>\\sigma(j_k)=k\\).\n\nAny rectangle covering \\(t_k\\) must have its **leftmost** column equal to \\(k+1\\); otherwise it would contain the uncovered square \\((j_k,k)\\).  \nConsequently a rectangle cannot contain two distinct squares \\(t_k\\) and \\(t_{k'}\\) with \\(k\\neq k'\\).  \nSince there are \\(n-1\\) such squares, we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles covering }R\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Global lower bound\n\nCombining (3) and (5) with (1) yields\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the **anti\u2011diagonal permutation**\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=n+1-i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThen the uncovered squares are \\((i,n+1-i)\\).  \nFor this permutation\n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+j\\le n\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+j\\ge n+2\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Left region.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define\n\n\\[\nL_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-k,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_k\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-k)\\times k\\).  \nIf \\((i,j)\\in L\\) then \\(i+j\\le n\\); setting \\(k=j\\) gives \\(i\\le n-j=n-k\\), so \\((i,j)\\in L_k\\).  \nThus the sets \\(\\{L_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) form a partition of \\(L\\) into exactly \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n*Right region.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define\n\n\\[\nR_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;n+1-k\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n\\(R_k\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-k)\\times k\\).  \nIf \\((i,j)\\in R\\) then \\(i+j\\ge n+2\\); letting \\(k=n+1-j\\) yields \\(k+1\\le i\\le n\\) and \\(n+1-k\\le j\\le n\\), so \\((i,j)\\in R_k\\).  \nHence \\(\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) partitions \\(R\\) into another \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\nAltogether we have \\( (n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)=4048\\) rectangular tiles, and each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square (the anti\u2011diagonal points).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (6) and the explicit construction both give the same number of tiles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing, verify that:\n\n* All mathematical statements are enclosed in TeX delimiters.  \n* The lower\u2011bound argument correctly shows that each of the \\(n-1\\) distinguished squares in \\(L\\) (resp. \\(R\\)) forces a distinct tile, using the \u201crightmost column\u201d (resp. \u201cleftmost column\u201d) property.  \n* The construction uses the anti\u2011diagonal permutation and the families \\(\\{L_k\\}\\) and \\(\\{R_k\\}\\) indeed partition the left and right regions without overlap.  \n* The final answer is presented as \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).  \n\nAll steps are now complete and rigorously justified."
