\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nReorder the rows so that  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}<\\ell_{2}<\\dots <\\ell_{n}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(We may reorder rows arbitrarily; a rectangle partition is unaffected by a permutation of rows.)  \nUnder this ordering, row \\(k\\) contains exactly the cells \\((k,j)\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le\\ell_k\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nL_\\sigma = \\bigl\\{(k,j)\\mid 1\\le k\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le\\ell_k\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nis precisely the Young diagram of shape  \n\n\\[\n(\\ell_n,\\ell_{n-1},\\dots ,\\ell_1)=(n-1,n-2,\\dots ,1,0).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L_\\sigma\\) is the staircase diagram consisting of the cells \\((k,j)\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k-1\\) (after the relabelling (1)).  \nThe same argument applied to the numbers \\(r_i=n-\\sigma(i)\\) shows that, after ordering rows by decreasing \\(\\sigma(i)\\), the set \\(R_\\sigma\\) is also a staircase diagram of shape \\((n-1,n-2,\\dots ,1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for a staircase diagram\n\nConsider a staircase diagram \\(S\\) of shape \\((n-1,n-2,\\dots ,1)\\).  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\np_k=(k,k-1)\n\\]\n\nbe the right\u2011most cell of row \\(k\\).  \nThe cells \\(\\{p_k\\mid k=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) lie in distinct columns (column \\(k-1\\)).\n\n**Claim.** No axis\u2011parallel rectangle contained in \\(S\\) can contain two distinct cells \\(p_{k}\\) and \\(p_{\\ell}\\) with \\(k<\\ell\\).\n\n*Proof of claim.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(R\\subseteq S\\) contains \\(p_k\\) and \\(p_\\ell\\) with \\(k<\\ell\\).  \nSince \\(R\\) is a product of a row interval and a column interval, it must also contain the cell \\((k,\\ell-1)\\) (row \\(k\\) and column \\(\\ell-1\\)).  \nBut \\(\\ell-1\\ge k\\), so column \\(\\ell-1\\) lies to the right of the right\u2011most cell of row \\(k\\); consequently \\((k,\\ell-1)\\notin S\\).  \nThis contradicts \\(R\\subseteq S\\). \u220e\n\nBecause the \\(n-1\\) cells \\(\\{p_k\\}\\) are pairwise forced into distinct rectangles, any rectangle partition of \\(S\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nApplying the same argument to the left\u2011most cells of each row of a staircase shows that any rectangle partition of a staircase also needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles when the rectangles are required to lie entirely to the left of the holes.  \n\nHence for any permutation \\(\\sigma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{(minimum number of rectangles covering }L_\\sigma\\text{)}\\ge n-1,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{(minimum number of rectangles covering }R_\\sigma\\text{)}\\ge n-1.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince the two regions are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board needs at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \nThen\n\n\\[\nL_\\sigma=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\},\\qquad\nR_\\sigma=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Tiles covering \\(L_\\sigma\\).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place one tile  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{\\text{left}}=\\{(i,c)\\mid i>c\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_c^{\\text{left}}\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-c)\\times1\\); it lies entirely in \\(L_\\sigma\\) because every row \\(i>c\\) satisfies \\(c<i\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{T_c^{\\text{left}}\\mid 1\\le c\\le n-1\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) disjoint tiles and covers all cells of \\(L_\\sigma\\).\n\n*Tiles covering \\(R_\\sigma\\).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) place one tile  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{\\text{right}}=\\{(i,c)\\mid i<c\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_c^{\\text{right}}\\) is a rectangle of size \\((c-1)\\times1\\); it lies entirely in \\(R_\\sigma\\) because every row \\(i<c\\) satisfies \\(c>i\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{T_c^{\\text{right}}\\mid 2\\le c\\le n\\}\\) consists of another \\(n-1\\) disjoint tiles and covers all cells of \\(R_\\sigma\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint (they occupy different columns) and together cover every unit square except the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), which are precisely the uncovered squares required.  \nThus we have a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (5) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the explicit construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing, verify that:\n\n1. The \u201cSummary\u201d contains a clear verdict with the boxed answer and a concise method sketch, mentioning the permutation\u2011matrix reformulation, the staircase shape of each side, the lower\u2011bound argument using right\u2011most cells, and the explicit diagonal construction.\n2. The \u201cDetailed Solution\u201d presents a complete, step\u2011by\u2011step proof:\n   - Reformulation to a permutation \\(\\sigma\\).\n   - Proof that each side \\(L_\\sigma\\) and \\(R_\\sigma\\) is a staircase diagram of shape \\((n-1,n-2,\\dots ,1)\\) after appropriate row ordering.\n   - Rigorous lower\u2011bound proof that any rectangle partition of a staircase needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles, using the pairwise\u2011incompatibility of the right\u2011most cells.\n   - Construction for \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) achieving exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n   - Final numeric evaluation for \\(n=2025\\).\n3. All mathematical expressions are enclosed in TeX delimiters.\n4. No extraneous commentary, alternative attempts, or unresolved steps appear.\n5. The final answer is correctly boxed as \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
