tains the cell \\((k,\\ell-1)\\). Since \\(\\ell-1\\ge k\\), column \\(\\ell-1\\) lies to the right of the right\u2011most cell of row \\(k\\), so \\((k,\\ell-1)\\) is outside the staircase \u2013 a contradiction. \u220e  \n\n   The \\(n-1\\) cells \\(\\{p_k\\}\\) must therefore lie in distinct rectangles, implying that any rectangle partition of a staircase uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles. Applying this to both \\(L_\\sigma\\) and \\(R_\\sigma\\) yields the universal lower bound  \n\n   \\[\n   \\text{#tiles}\\ \\ge\\ (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   L_\\sigma=\\{(i,j):j<i\\},\\qquad R_\\sigma=\\{(i,j):j>i\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Tiles covering \\(L_\\sigma\\).*  \n   For each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n   \\[\n   T_c^{\\text{left}}=\\{(i,c):i>c\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a rectangle of size \\((n-c)\\times1\\).  Every cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<i\\) belongs to \\(T_j^{\\text{left}}\\); thus the family \\(\\{T_c^{\\text{left}}\\}_{c=1}^{n-1}\\) (exactly \\(n-1\\) tiles) covers all of \\(L_\\sigma\\).\n\n   *Tiles covering \\(R_\\sigma\\).*  \n   For each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n   \\[\n   T_c^{\\text{right}}=\\{(i,c):i<c\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a rectangle of size \\((c-1)\\times1\\).  Every cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>i\\) belongs to \\(T_j^{\\text{right}}\\); the family \\(\\{T_c^{\\text{right}}\\}_{c=2}^{n}\\) (again \\(n-1\\) tiles) covers all of \\(R_\\sigma\\).\n\n   The two families are disjoint (they occupy the same columns but disjoint rows), and together they cover every unit square except the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), which are precisely the required uncovered squares.  Hence we obtain a tiling with exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound (1) and the explicit construction both give the same value \\(2n-2\\).  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  A placement of axis\u2011parallel rectangular tiles on the \\(n\\times n\\) grid must satisfy:\n\n* each tile\u2019s sides lie on grid lines,\n* no two tiles overlap (each unit square is covered by at most one tile),\n* each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square.\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix\n\nSince each row contains exactly one uncovered square, there is a function \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) lies in column \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection, i.e. a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU_\\sigma=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Tiles must stay entirely on one side of the permutation\n\nConsider any tile \\(T\\).  If \\(T\\) contains a cell \\((i,j_1)\\) with \\(j_1<\\sigma(i)\\) (a cell left of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\)) and also a cell \\((i,j_2)\\) with \\(j_2>\\sigma(i)\\) (a cell right of it), then because \\(T\\) is a rectangle its column interval contains \\(\\sigma(i)\\); consequently \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\in T\\), contradicting the requirement that uncovered squares are not covered.  Hence for each row intersected by \\(T\\), all cells of \\(T\\) in that row lie either strictly left of \\(\\sigma(i)\\) or strictly right of \\(\\sigma(i)\\).\n\nSince the column interval of \\(T\\) is the same for all rows it meets, either\n\n\\[\n\\max\\{j:(i,j)\\in T\\}<\\min_{i\\in\\text{rows}(T)}\\sigma(i)\n\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\n\\min\\{j:(i,j)\\in T\\}>\\max_{i\\in\\text{rows}(T)}\\sigma(i).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(T\\) is completely contained either in  \n\n\\[\nL_\\sigma=\\{(i,j):j<\\sigma(i)\\}\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nR_\\sigma=\\{(i,j):j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently a tiling of the whole board is a partition of \\(L_\\sigma\\) into rectangles together with a partition of \\(R_\\sigma\\) into rectangles.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Shape of \\(L_\\sigma\\) (and of \\(R_\\sigma\\))\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=\\sigma(i)-1\\qquad(0\\le\\ell_i\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nReorder the rows so that  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}<\\ell_{2}<\\dots <\\ell_{n}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(Any permutation of rows does not affect the existence of a rectangle partition.)  \nAfter this reordering, row \\(k\\) contains exactly the cells \\((k,j)\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le\\ell_k\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nL_\\sigma=\\bigl\\{(k,j):1\\le k\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le\\ell_k\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nis precisely the Young diagram of shape  \n\n\\[\n(\\ell_n,\\ell_{n-1},\\dots ,\\ell_1)=(n-1,n-2,\\dots ,1,0).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L_\\sigma\\) is a staircase consisting of the cells \\((k,j)\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k-1\\) (after the ordering (4)).  \n\nApplying the same reasoning to the numbers \\(r_i=n-\\sigma(i)\\) (the number of cells to the right of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\)) shows that, after ordering rows by decreasing \\(\\sigma(i)\\), the set \\(R_\\sigma\\) is also a staircase of shape \\((n-1,n-2,\\dots ,1)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Lower bound for a staircase diagram\n\nConsider a staircase \\(S\\) of shape \\((n-1,n-2,\\dots ,1)\\).  For each row \\(k\\) with \\(k\\ge2\\) let  \n\n\\[\np_k=(k,k-1)\n\\]\n\nbe the right\u2011most cell of that row.  The set \\(\\{p_k\\mid k=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) contains \\(n-1\\) cells, each in a distinct column.\n\n**Lemma.** No axis\u2011parallel rectangle contained in \\(S\\) can contain two distinct cells \\(p_k\\) and \\(p_\\ell\\) with \\(k<\\ell\\).\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a rectangle \\(R\\subseteq S\\) contains \\(p_k\\) and \\(p_\\ell\\) (\\(k<\\ell\\)).  By definition of a rectangle, its column interval contains both columns \\(k-1\\) and \\(\\ell-1\\); consequently it also contains the cell \\((k,\\ell-1)\\).  Since \\(\\ell-1\\ge k\\), column \\(\\ell-1\\) lies strictly to the right of the right\u2011most cell of row \\(k\\), so \\((k,\\ell-1)\\notin S\\).  This contradicts \\(R\\subseteq S\\). \u220e\n\nBecause each rectangle can contain at most one of the \\(n-1\\) cells \\(\\{p_k\\}\\), any rectangle partition of \\(S\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nApplying this lemma to \\(L_\\sigma\\) (which, after the row ordering (4), is a staircase) shows that any rectangle partition of \\(L_\\sigma\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.  By the same argument applied to \\(R_\\sigma\\) we obtain another lower bound of \\(n-1\\) tiles.  Since the two regions are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board needs at least\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Construction achieving the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  Then the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nL_\\sigma=\\{(i,j):j<i\\},\\qquad\nR_\\sigma=\\{(i,j):j>i\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Covering \\(L_\\sigma\\).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{\\text{left}}=\\{(i,c):i>c\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a rectangle of size \\((n-c)\\times1\\).  The family \\(\\{T_c^{\\text{left}}\\}_{c=1}^{n-1}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) disjoint tiles and covers every cell of \\(L_\\sigma\\): a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<i\\) belongs to \\(T_j^{\\text{left}}\\).\n\n*Covering \\(R_\\sigma\\).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{\\text{right}}=\\{(i,c):i<c\\},\n\\]\n\na rectangle of size \\((c-1)\\times1\\).  The family \\(\\{T_c^{\\text{right}}\\}_{c=2}^{n}\\) also contains \\(n-1\\) disjoint tiles and covers every cell of \\(R_\\sigma\\): a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>i\\) belongs to \\(T_j^{\\text{right}}\\).\n\nThe two families occupy the same columns but disjoint rows, so they do not overlap.  Together they cover all unit squares except the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), which are precisely the required uncovered squares.  Hence we have a tiling with exactly\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe universal lower bound (6) and the explicit construction both give the same value \\(2n-2\\).  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles for an \\(n\\times n\\) board is \\(2n-2\\).  For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nThus the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing, verify that:\n\n1. The **Summary** states the verdict with the boxed answer and provides a concise method sketch covering the permutation\u2011matrix reformulation, separation into left/right regions, staircase shape, lower\u2011bound lemma, and the explicit construction.\n2. The **Detailed Solution** presents a complete, step\u2011by\u2011step proof:\n   - Reformulation to a permutation \\(\\sigma\\).\n   - Argument that each tile lies wholly in \\(L_\\sigma\\) or \\(R_\\sigma\\).\n   - Proof that \\(L_\\sigma\\) (and \\(R_\\sigma\\)) is a staircase diagram after appropriate row ordering.\n   - Rigorous lemma showing any rectangle in a staircase can contain at most one of the right\u2011most cells, yielding the lower bound \\(2n-2\\).\n   - Explicit construction for \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) achieving exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n   - Final numeric evaluation for \\(n=2025\\).\n3. All mathematical expressions are enclosed in TeX delimiters.\n4. No extraneous commentary, alternative attempts, or unresolved steps appear.\n5. The final answer is correctly boxed as \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
