\n#### Lemma 2 (At least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to tile \\(L\\)).  \n\n*Proof.*  \nReorder the rows so that the values \\(\\sigma(i)\\) are increasing:\n\\[\n\\sigma(1)<\\sigma(2)<\\dots <\\sigma(n).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n(If necessary we rename the rows; this does not affect the existence of a tiling.)\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) define its *left length*\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=\\sigma(i)-1 .\n\\]\nBecause of (2) we have\n\\[\n0=\\ell_1<\\ell_2<\\dots <\\ell_n=n-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nThus the rows with \\(\\ell_i>0\\) are exactly the rows \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\); each of these rows contains a positive number of cells of \\(L\\), and the numbers \\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) are pairwise distinct, taking all values \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\nConsider any rectangle \\(Q=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) that lies inside \\(L\\).  \nSince \\(Q\\subseteq L\\), for each row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) we must have \\(c\\ge 1\\) and \\(d\\le \\ell_i\\) (otherwise a cell of \\(Q\\) would be to the right of the hole of that row).  \nIf two rows \\(i<i'\\) with \\(\\ell_i<\\ell_{i'}\\) both belong to \\([a,b]\\), then\n\\[\nc\\le d\\le \\ell_{i'}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad c>\\ell_i .\n\\]\nConsequently the integer \\(\\ell_i+1=\\sigma(i)\\) lies in the interval \\([c,d]\\), which would place the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) inside \\(Q\\) – impossible.  \nHence a rectangle contained in \\(L\\) can involve **only rows having the same left length**.\n\nBecause the positive left lengths \\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) are all distinct, each rectangle can cover cells from at most one of the rows \\(2,\\dots ,n\\).  \nTherefore at least one rectangle is required for each of these \\(n-1\\) rows, i.e. at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to tile \\(L\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (At least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to tile \\(R\\)).  \n\n*Proof.*  \nThe right side \\(R\\) is symmetric to \\(L\\): after the same ordering (2) the *right length* of ro