 cell \\((i',j')\\in R\\).  \nThen \\(j<\\sigma(i)\\) and \\(j'>\\sigma(i')\\).  \nBecause the column interval \\([c,d]\\) of \\(Q\\) contains both \\(j\\) and \\(j'\\), it also contains every integer between them.  \nIf \\(\\sigma(i)\\le j'\\) then \\(\\sigma(i)\\in[c,d]\\); the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\in U\\) would lie in \\(Q\\), contradicting the requirement that uncovered squares are not covered.  \nOtherwise \\(\\sigma(i) > j'\\); then \\(\\sigma(i')<j\\) (since \\(j'>\\sigma(i')\\) and \\(j<\\sigma(i)\\)), and similarly \\(\\sigma(i')\\in[c,d]\\), again a contradiction.  \nHence such a rectangle cannot exist. \u220e\n\nConsequently any tiling of the board by axis\u2011parallel rectangles that avoids all uncovered squares consists of a tiling of \\(L\\) together with a tiling of \\(R\\).  If \\(T_L\\) (resp. \\(T_R\\)) denotes the number of tiles used for \\(L\\) (resp. \\(R\\)), the total number of tiles is\n\\[\nT = T_L + T_R . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (At least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for \\(L\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) consider the cell\n\\[\nr_i = (i,\\sigma(i)-1),\n\\]\nthe **rightmost** cell of that row that lies in \\(L\\).  \nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) such cells, because the unique row with \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) has no cell in \\(L\\).\n\nSuppose a rectangle \\(Q\\subseteq L\\) contains two distinct rightmost cells \\(r_i\\) and \\(r_{i'}\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<\\sigma(i')\\).  \nSince \\(Q\\) is a rectangle, its column interval contains both \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i')-1\\), and therefore also the integer \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nBut \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) is an uncovered square, contradicting \\(Q\\subseteq L\\).  \nThus a rectangle inside \\(L\\) can contain **at most one** of the rightmost cells.\n\nBecause the \\(n-1\\) rightmost cells are all distinct, covering them requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  Hence \\(T_L\\ge n-1\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (At least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for \\(R\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe argument is symmetric.  For each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) consider the **leftmost** cell of that row inside \\(R\\):\n\\[\n\\ell_i = (i,\\sigma(i)+1).\n\\]\nAgain there are exactly \\(n-1\\) such cells (the row with \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) contributes none).  \n\nIf a rectangle \\(Q\\subseteq R\\) contained two distinct cells \\(\\ell_i,\\ell_{i'}\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<\\sigma(i')\\), then the column interval of \\(Q\\) would contain \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i')+1\\), and therefore also the integer \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\).  This integer is the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i)+1-1)=(i,\\sigma(i))\\), a contradiction.  Hence each rectangle in \\(R\\) contains at most one \\(\\ell_i\\), and at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover all \\(\\ell_i\\).  Thus \\(T_R\\ge n-1\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound for the whole board\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 and Lemma\u202f3,\n\\[\nT_L\\ge n-1,\\qquad T_R\\ge n-1 .\n\\]\nUsing (3) we obtain the universal lower bound\n\\[\nT \\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  Then the uncovered squares are the main diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Tiles covering the left side \\(L\\).*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle\n\\[\nQ^{\\text{left}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\nThis rectangle consists of the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\); exactly the cells of column \\(j\\) that lie left of the diagonal.  The \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{Q^{\\text{left}}_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover the whole set \\(L\\).\n\n*Tiles covering the right side \\(R\\).*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle\n\\[\nQ^{\\text{right}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nThese are the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\), i.e. the cells of column \\(j\\) that lie right of the diagonal.  The \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{Q^{\\text{right}}_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) are disjoint from each other and from the left\u2011side rectangles, and together they cover the whole set \\(R\\).\n\nEvery unit square of the board is either on the diagonal (uncovered) or belongs to exactly one of the rectangles described above.  Hence the construction uses precisely\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\ntiles and satisfies all the required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (4) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the explicit construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
