on. ∎  \n\n   Hence each rectangle can cover **at most one** element of \\(S\\).  \n   Since \\(|S|=2(n-1)=2n-2\\), any tiling of the board must contain at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.  \n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient, and the counting argument shows that fewer tiles are impossible. Therefore the minimum number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) we obtain \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n**Final answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---  \n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  We are given an \\(n\\times n\\) board of unit squares.  A *tile* is an axis‑aligned rectangle whose sides lie on the grid lines; a tile may consist of any positive integer number of unit squares.  Tiles may not overlap, i.e. each unit square is covered by at most one tile.  The requirement is that every row and every column contain **exactly one** uncovered unit square.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to the identity permutation  \n\nLet the uncovered squares be \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) for a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nIf we permute the rows by the map \\(i\\mapsto i\\) (no change) and permute the columns by the map \\(j\\mapsto \\pi^{-1}(j)\\), the uncovered squares become \\((i,i)\\) for all \\(i\\).  Such a simultaneous permutation of rows and columns sends any rectangle to a rectangle of the same shape, and preserves the condition “one uncovered square per row and per column”.  Consequently the minimal number of tiles is the same for every permutation; we may assume from now on that the uncovered squares are precisely the main diagonal cells\n\\[\nD=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n* a **horizontal tile**\n  \\[\n  H_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n