     \\[\n     L_j=\\{(i,j):i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,n-1.\n     \\]\n   - Tile the right region by the vertical rectangles  \n     \\[\n     R_j=\\{(i,j):i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\},\\qquad j=2,\\dots ,n.\n     \\]\n   Each rectangle is a contiguous block of unit squares, contains no diagonal square, and the collection of all \\(2n-2\\) rectangles covers every non‑diagonal square exactly once.  \n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles coincide, so the minimum number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying “each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square’’ forces the set of uncovered squares to be a **permutation matrix**: there is a unique uncovered square in each row and each column.  \nHence there exists a permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) the covered squares split into two contiguous blocks:\n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j<p(i)\\},\\qquad \nR_i=\\{(i,j):p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the **left region** \\(L=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\) and the **right region** \\(R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i\\).  \nClearly \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\) and \\(L\\cup R\\) is exactly the set of covered unit squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1.  Every tile lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(T\\) contains a square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<p(i)\\) (hence \\((i,j)\\in L_i\\)) and also a square \\((i,k)\\) with \\(k>p(i)\\) (hence \\((i,k)\\in R_i\\)).  \nSince the column interval of \\(T\\) is contiguous, it contains all columns between \\(j\\) and \\(k\\), in particular column \\(p(i)\\).  \nThus \\((i,p(i))\\) would belong to \\(T\\), contradicting the requirement that no tile may cover an uncovered square. ∎\n\nConsequently a tiling of the whole board is the disjoint union of a tiling of \\(L\\) and a tiling of \\(R\\).  \nThe total number of tiles equals the number of tiles covering \\(L\\) plus the number covering \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound for the left region\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=|L_i|=p(i)-1\\qquad (0\\le\\ell_i\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); exactly one row has \\(\\ell_i=0\\) (the row whose hole lies in column 1).\n\nFor every \\(\\ell\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let \\(i_\\ell\\) be the unique row with \\(\\ell_{i_\\ell}=\\ell\\).  \nThe square \\((i_\\ell,\\ell)\\) is the **rightmost** covered square of row \\(i_\\ell\\).\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Claim.}\\quad\\text{Any rectangle covering }(i_\\ell,\\ell)\\text{ has rightmost column exactly }\\ell .\n\\]\n\n*Proof of claim.*  \nIf a rectangle \\(T\\) covering \\((i_\\ell,\\ell)\\) had rightmost column \\(c<\\ell\\), then column \\(\\ell\\) would not belong to \\(T\\), contradicting that \\((i_\\ell,\\ell)\\in T\\).  \nIf \\(c>\\ell\\), then column \\(\\ell+1\\) belongs to \\(T\\).  \nBut column \\(\\ell+1\\) is precisely the hole \\((i_\\ell,\\ell+1)\\) of row \\(i_\\ell\\), which is forbidden. ∎\n\nThus each value \\(\\ell\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) forces a distinct rectangle in any tiling of \\(L\\).  \nHence any tiling of \\(L\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound for the right region\n\nDefine for each row \\(i\\)\n\n\\[\nr_i:=|R_i|=n-p(i)\\qquad (0\\le r_i\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(\\{r_i\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); exactly one row has \\(r_i=0\\) (the row whose hole lies in column \\(n\\)).  \nFor each \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let \\(j_r\\) be the unique row with \\(r_{j_r}=r\\).  \