) the hole in that row lies at column \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nCondition (1) implies that either  \n\n\\[\nC\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\sigma(i)-1\\}\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad \nC\\subseteq\\{\\sigma(i)+1,\\dots ,n\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf for two rows \\(i,i'\\in R\\) the two alternatives in (2) were different, then the column interval \\(C\\) would have to contain both a number \\(\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\) and a number \\(\\ge\\sigma(i')+1\\); consequently it would contain \\(\\sigma(i)\\) or \\(\\sigma(i')\\) and would violate (1).  \nHence the same alternative holds for **all** rows of \\(R\\).  \n\nConsequently every admissible rectangle is of one of the two types:\n\n* **Left‑justified:** there exists a number \\(c\\) such that  \n  \\[\n  C\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,c\\},\\qquad c<\\min_{i\\in R}\\sigma(i). \\tag{L}\n  \\]\n  Its rightmost column is \\(c\\).\n\n* **Right‑justified:** there exists a number \\(d\\) such that  \n  \\[\n  C\\subseteq\\{d,d+1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad d>\\max_{i\\in R}\\sigma(i). \\tag{R}\n  \\]\n  Its leftmost column is \\(d\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound\n\nFor each column \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\ni_k=\\sigma^{-1}(k+1).\n\\]\n\nThe square \\((i_k,k)\\) belongs to the set of squares that must be covered, because \\(k\\neq\\sigma(i_k)=k+1\\).  \nSince column \\(k\\) is **left** of the hole in row \\(i_k\\), any rectangle containing \\((i_k,k)\\) must be left‑justified.  \nBy (L) its rightmost column equals \\(k\\).  \nThus **every** column \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) is the rightmost column of a distinct left‑justified rectangle.\n\nDually, for each column \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nj_k=\\sigma^{-1}(k-1).\n\\]\n\nThe square \\((j_k,k)\\) lies **right** of the hole in row \\(j_k\\); any rectangle covering it must be right‑justified, and by (R) its leftmost column equals \\(k\\).  \nHence **every** column \\(k\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is the leftmost column of a distinct right‑justified rectangle.\n\nA rectangle cannot be simultaneously left‑ and right‑justified (otherwise its column set would be empty), so the two families are disjoint.  \nTherefore any tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nDefine the following rectangles:\n\n* For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) let  \n\n  \\[\n  H_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n  \\]\n  a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle.  \n  It is right‑justified because all its columns are \\(>i=\\sigma(i)\\).\n\n* For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) let  \n\n  \\[\n  V_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n  \\]\n  a \\((n-k)\\times1\\) rectangle.  \n  It is left‑justified because all its columns are \\(\\le k<\\sigma(i)=i\\) for every row \\(i\\) it contains.\n\nThe rectangles \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint (they occupy different rows), and the rectangles \\(\\{V_k\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint (they occupy different columns).  \nMoreover a horizontal rectangle \\(H_i\\) never meets a vertical rectangle \\(V_k\\) because any common square would be \\((i,k)\\) with \\(k\\le i\\), which is a hole \\((i,i)\\) only when \\(k=i\\); but \\(V_i\\) does not contain row \\(i\\) (its rows start at \\(i+1\\)).  \nHence all \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are disjoint.\n\nEvery square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j\\neq i\\) belongs either to \\(H_i\\) (if \\(j>i\\)) or to \\(V_j\\) (if \\(j<i\\)).  \nThus the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles cover **all** squares except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), exactly the required uncovered squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound \\(2n-2\\) holds for any placement of the uncovered squares, and the construction above shows that the bound is attainable.  \nConsequently the minimal po