xt{ by rectangles requires at least }n-1\\text{ rectangles.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Lower bound for the upper region \\(U\\)\n\nDefine the symmetric set\n\\[\nT=\\{(i,i+1):1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\subset U .\n\\]\nExactly the same argument as in Lemma 2 shows that a rectangle contained in \\(U\\) can contain at most one element of \\(T\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Corollary 4.}\\qquad\\text{Any tiling of }U\\text{ by rectangles requires at least }n-1\\text{ rectangles.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Global lower bound  \n\nCombining Corollaries 3 and 4 and using Lemma 1 (which forces the rectangles for \\(L\\) and \\(U\\) to be disjoint families) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every admissible tiling uses at least }2n-2\\text{ rectangles}.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Construction attaining the bound  \n\nFor each row index \\(i\\) define the following rectangles:\n\n*If \\(i>1\\):*  \n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) rectangle lying entirely in \\(L\\).\n\n*If \\(i<n\\):*  \n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j):i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) rectangle lying entirely in \\(U\\).\n\nThe collection\n\\[\n\\mathcal{T}=\\{L_i\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\}\\cup\\{R_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\ncontains exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n*Verification of coverage.*  \nTake any square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j\\neq i\\).  \n- If \\(j<i\\), then by definition \\((i,j)\\in L_i\\).  \n- If \\(j>i\\), then \\((i,j)\\in R_i\\).  \n\nThus every non‑diagonal square belongs to a unique rectangle of \\(\\mathcal{T}\\).  \nSince each rectangle is wholly contained in either \\(L\\) or \\(U\\), no rectangle contains a diagonal square.  \nMoreover, distinct rectangles occupy distinct rows, so they are pairwise disjoint.  \n\nHence \\(\\mathcal{T}\\) is a valid tiling of the board with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7. Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved a lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles for any admissible tiling and exhibited an explicit tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles. Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
