(i)\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal L\\cap\\mathcal R=\\varnothing .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Every rectangle lies completely in \\(\\mathcal L\\) or in \\(\\mathcal R\\)\n\nLet a rectangle be the product of two intervals  \n\n\\[\nI=[a,b]\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,N\\},\\qquad \nJ=[c,d]\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,N\\},\n\\]\n\nso it consists of the cells \\(\\{(i,j):i\\in I,\\;j\\in J\\}\\).\n\nAssume the rectangle contains no uncovered cell.\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\in I\\) the hole lies at column \\(p(i)\\).\nSince the rectangle avoids that hole, either  \n\n\\[\nd<p(i)\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad c>p(i).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf there exist rows \\(i_1,i_2\\in I\\) with \\(p(i_1)\\le d<p(i_2)\\),\nthen (1) cannot hold for both rows simultaneously, contradicting the\nassumption that the rectangle is hole‑free.\nConsequently either  \n\n\\[\nd<\\min_{i\\in I}p(i) \\quad\\text{or}\\quad c>\\max_{i\\in I}p(i).\n\\]\n\nThus the whole rectangle lies **to the left** of all holes in its rows\n(\\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,\\min p(i)-1\\}\\)), i.e. inside \\(\\mathcal L\\),\nor **to the right** of all holes (\\(J\\subseteq\\{\\max p(i)+1,\\dots ,N\\}\\)),\ni.e. inside \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\nHence the tiling of the board splits into a tiling of \\(\\mathcal L\\) and a\ntiling of \\(\\mathcal R\\) separately.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the left region  \n\nThe numbers \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_N\\) form a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,N-1\\}\\):\nindeed \\(L_i=p(i)-1\\) and \\(p\\) is a permutation.\n\nFor each column  \n\n\\[\nc\\in\\{1,\\dots ,N-1\\}\n\\]\n\nlet  \n\n\\[\nr_c:=p^{-1}(c+1).\n\\]\n\nThen \\(L_{r_c}=c\\); consequently the cell \\((r_c,c)\\) belongs to\n\\(\\mathcal L\\), and column \\(c+1\\) does **not** contain this row, i.e.\n\\((r_c,c+1)\\notin\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThe cell \\((r_c,c)\\) is the **leftmost** cell of row \\(r_c\\) that lies in\n\\(\\mathcal L\\)