 So the lower bound for left region still holds: at least N-1 rectangles needed to cover all left region cells, because each rectangle can cover at most one of the c_i. However, we need to verify that covering all left region cells necessarily requires covering each c_i. Indeed, each left region cell is either a c_i (rightmost cell) or lies left of c_i in its row. So any rectangle covering left region cells must also cover the c_i for each row that has left region cells. Since each row with L_i>0 has at least one left region cell, that row's c_i must be covered by some rectangle. So we need at least N-1 rectangles. So the lower bound for left region holds even if some rectangles cross between left and right region.\n\nNow, does the lower bound for right region also hold? The argument is symmetric: define rightmost cells of right region? Actually, they define right region cells as those to the right of the hole. They use symmetry to argue similarly that at least N-1 rectangles needed to cover right region. The same reasoning can be applied: define for each row i with R_i>0 (i.e., p(i) < N) the leftmost cell of right region (i, p(i)+1). Those are distinct columns (since p(i) distinct). Then each rectangle can contain at most one such cell. So at least N-1 rectangles needed for right region. That holds.\n\nThus the lower bound of 2(N-1) holds even if some rectangles cross between left and right region. However, we need to verify that the argument for right region also holds: The right region cells are (i, p(i)+1) for each row i where p(i) < N. Since p(i) are a permutation of 1..N, there is exactly one row with p(i)=N, thus N-1 rows have p(i) < N. So there are N-1 such cells. They are distinct because p(i) distinct. For each such cell, any rectangle can contain at most one of them, by analogous reasoning: if a rectangle contains two such cells (i1, p(i1)+1) and (i2, p(i2)+1) with p(i1) < p(i2), then the rectangle's column interval must contain column p(i1)+1 and p(i2)+1