d right region. So the lower bounds for left and right region may not be additive. The total number of tiles might be as low as N-1, but we need to check if we can achieve that.\n\nThus the solution's lower bound of 2(N-1) might be invalid because it assumes disjointness. However, we need to examine if a tiling with fewer than 2(N-1) tiles is possible. Let's try to find a tiling with fewer tiles for N=2025 (or general N). Could we tile the complement of a permutation matrix with N tiles? Or maybe N-1 tiles? Let's explore.\n\nWe have N holes (one per row and column). The complement has N(N-1) cells. If we could tile it with N tiles, each tile would have average area (N(N-1))/N = N-1 cells per tile. That's plausible. The solution uses 2(N-1) tiles, each tile average area (N(N-1))/(2(N-1)) = N/2 cells per tile. So maybe we can do better.\n\nConsider the identity permutation. The complement consists of two triangles. Could we tile the whole complement with N tiles? For example, we could tile each row i (i > 1) with a horizontal rectangle covering all cells in that row except the hole at column i. That would be a rectangle of length N-1 (covering columns 1..i-1 and i+1..N). But that rectangle is not contiguous because it would have a hole in the middle. So not allowed. So we need to split each row into two rectangles: left part (columns 1..i-1) and right part (columns i+1..N). So each row would need at least 2 rectangles unless one of the parts is empty (i.e., i=1 or i=N). So we need at least 2(N-2) + 2 = 2N-2 rectangles? Actually, for i=1, left part empty, right part columns 2..N (N-1 cells) can be covered by a single rectangle. For i=N, right part empty, left part columns 1..N-1 (N-1 cells) can be covered by a single rectangle. For rows i=2..N-1, both left and right parts non-empty, requiring at least 2 rectangles per row? However, we could combine left parts of multiple rows into a single rectangle that spans multiple rows, as the solution does. So the lower bound per