terval would be a set of columns that are all less than p(i1) and all greater than p(i2). That is possible only if p(i2) < p(i1) and there are columns between them. But the rectangle's column interval must be contiguous, so it can be any interval [c,d] with c <= d. The condition c > p(i2) and d < p(i1) yields c > p(i2) and d < p(i1). Since c <= d, we need p(i2) < p(i1) and there exists an integer interval (c,d) that lies between them. That's possible if p(i2)+1 <= p(i1)-1. So the rectangle could be placed entirely between the two holes. However, the rectangle also includes rows i1 and i2. For row i1, the hole is at column p(i1). The rectangle's columns are all less than p(i1) (since d < p(i1)), so it's left of the hole for row i1. For row i2, rectangle's columns are all greater than p(i2) (since c > p(i2)), so it's right of the hole for row i2. So the rectangle straddles the holes: left of hole in one row, right of hole in another row. The solution claims this is impossible because the column interval is contiguous. But as we just reasoned, it's possible if the holes are at different columns and the rectangle's column interval lies between them. However, does that rectangle contain any hole? Let's examine: For row i1, the hole is at column p(i1). Since rectangle's columns are all less than p(i1), it does not contain the hole. For row i2, rectangle's columns are all greater than p(i2), so does not contain the hole. So the rectangle does not contain any hole. So the argument that such a rectangle cannot exist might be flawed. But the solution's later argument uses the fact that each tile must be wholly on one side of all holes in its rows. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe need to verify the claim: For a rectangle I x J, if there exist rows i1,i2 in I such that for i1 the rectangle lies left of the hole (i.e., d < p(i1)), and for i2 the rectangle lies right of the hole (c > p(i2)), then that rectangle cannot exist because J is contiguous. Actually, we need to consid