uld be left of the diagonal for some rows and right of the diagonal for others, as long as the column interval J lies between the smallest and largest row indices? Actually, the condition is: For each i in I, either J is left of i (i.e., d < i) or J is right of i (c > i). The rectangle can have J left of i for some i and right of i for others. But for a given rectangle, J is fixed. So we need to find I and J such that for each i in I, either d < i or c > i. This is possible if J is a contiguous interval that does not intersect the set {i: i in I} (the set of row indices). But the condition is not about intersecting the set of row indices; it's about intersecting the diagonal cells (i,i). The rectangle cannot contain any cell (i,i). That means we need to avoid the diagonal cells. For each i in I, we need to avoid column i. So J must not contain i. So J must be a subset of {1,...,i-1} ∪ {i+1,...,N} for each i. So J cannot contain any i in I. So J must be a subset of columns that avoid all row indices in I. That is, J ∩ I = ∅. So J must be a set of columns that does not intersect the set of rows I. Since J is an interval, we need an interval that does not intersect I. So either J lies entirely to the left of min(I) (i.e., d < min(I)), or J lies entirely to the right of max(I) (i.e., c > max(I)). Because if J is an interval and I is also an interval (since rows are contiguous), then J cannot be partially left of some rows and partially right of others without intersecting some row index. Let's examine: I = [a,b] (contiguous rows). J = [c,d] (contiguous columns). Condition J ∩ I = ∅. Since both intervals are contiguous, the only way they can be disjoint is either d < a (J left of I) or c > b (J right of I). If J is between a and b (i.e., a <= c <= d <= b), then J intersects I. If J overlaps partially, e.g., c < a <= d < b, then J intersects I because d >= a. So the only way for J and I to be disjoint intervals is J entirely left of I or J entirely right of I. So the rectangle must be wholly left of the diagonal (i.e., columns less than all rows in I) or wholly right of the diagonal (columns greater than all rows in I). This is a stronger condition than the earlier analysis. Let's verify: The condition J ∩ I = ∅ is necessary because for each i in I, we need i ∉ J. So J cannot contain any integer i that is also in I. Since I is an interval of integers, J must avoid all those integers. Since J is also an interval, it cannot \"skip\" over them; it must be either entirely to the left or entirely to the right. So indeed, any rectangle cannot have J left of some rows and right of others; it must be wholly left of all rows or wholly right of all rows. However, earlier we gave an example where I = {1,2} (rows 1 and 2) and J = [3,4] (columns 3 and 4). Here I = [1,2], J = [3,4]. J is to the right of I (c=3 > b=2). So J is wholly right of I. That matches the condition. Our earlier example with I = {1,2} and J = [3,4] is indeed wholly right of I. That is consistent with the condition. The earlier example with I = {1,2} and J = [3,4] is okay. But we gave an example where J = [3,4] and holes at p(1)=5, p(2)=2. Wait, p(2)=2, so row 2 hole is at column 2. J = [3,4] is to the right of row 2 (c=3 > 2). For row 1, hole at column 5, J = [3,4] is left of hole (d=4 < 5). So J is left of hole for row 1 and right of hole for row 2. But J is still to the right of row 2 (since row 2 index = 2). So J is to the right of all rows in I? Actually, I = {1,2}. The maximum row index is 2. J = [3,4] has c=3 > 2, so J is to the right of all rows. So J is wholly right of I. So the rectangle is wholly right of the diagonal for all rows in I (since the diagonal cells are at (i,i), and J's columns are > i for each i in I). Indeed, for row 1, J's columns are 3,4, which are > 1, so J is right of the diagonal for row 1. For row 2, J's columns are 3,4, which are > 2, so J is right of the diagonal for row 2. So the rectangle lies entirely in the right region for all rows. So the earlier claim that the rectangle is left of hole for row 1 is not correct: The hole for row 1 is at column 5, but J = [3,4] is left of 5, but still J is right of row index 1, so it's still in the right region (since right region is defined as columns > p(i) or columns > i? Wait, right region is defined as columns > p(i). Actually, the right region is defined as columns > p(i), not > i. So for row 1, p(1) = 5, J = [3,4] is not > p(1). So J is not in right region for row 1. But the earlier argument about region L and R is based on p(i), not i. So the rectangle is left of hole for row 1, right of hole for row 2. So the rectangle is not wholly in left region (since for row 2 it's right of hole) nor wholly in right region (since for row 1 it's left of hole). However, does that contradict the condition J ∩ I = ∅? Let's check: I = {1,2}, J = {3,4}. I = {1,2}, J = {3,4}. They are disjoint intervals. So J ∩ I = ∅. So the rectangle does not contain any hole (i,i). But does it contain any hole (i,p(i))? The holes are at (1,5) and (2,2). The rectangle includes columns 3,4, rows 1,2. So it does not include (1,5) because column 5 not in J. It does not include (2,2) because column 2 not in J. So it's allowed. However, the rectangle includes cells (1,3), (1,4), (2,3), (2,4). For row 1, those cells are left of the hole (since hole at column 5). For row 2, those cells are right of the hole (hole at column 2). So the rectangle straddles left and right region across rows. So the earlier claim that any tile must be wholly in left region or wholly in right region is false. However, the condition J ∩ I = ∅ ensures that the rectangle does not intersect the diagonal (i,i). But the region L and R are defined relative to p(i), not i. So the rectangle can straddle left and right region relative to p(i) if p(i) are not monotonic with i.\n\nThus the solution's claim that any tile lies wholly in L or wholly in R is false. However, they later use this claim to argue that the tiling splits into left region tiling and right region tiling. Since it's false, the lower bound may not hold. But perhaps the lower bound still holds even if some tiles cross between L and R. We need to examine whether crossing tiles could reduce the number of tiles needed below 2(N-1). The lower bound argument uses the fact that each tile can cover at most one of the rightmost cells in left region (c_i). If a tile can cross between left and right region, could it cover two such rightmost cells? The rightmost cells c_i are defined as (i, L_i) where L_i = p(i)-1. These cells are the rightmost cells of left region in each row. If a tile crosses between left and right region, can it contain two such cells? Let's examine.\n\nSuppose we have two rows i1,i2 with L_i1 < L_i2 (since L_i are distinct). The cell c_i1 = (i1, L_i1) is the rightmost cell left of hole in row i1. The cell c_i2 = (i2, L_i2) is the rightmost cell left of hole in row i2. If a rectangle includes both cells, then its column interval J must contain both columns L_i1 and L_i2. Since L_i1 < L_i2, J must contain all columns between them, including L_i1+1. For row i1, column L_i1+1 is p(i1) (hole). So the rectangle would contain a hole, which is not allowed. So indeed, any rectangle cannot contain two distinct rightmost cells c_i. This holds regardless of whether the rectangle also contains cells on the right side of holes for other rows. Because the argument only uses the fact that the rectangle contains both columns L_i1 and L_i2, which implies it contains column L_i1+1, which is the hole for row i1. So any rectangle cannot contain two distinct rightmost cells c_i. So each rectangle can cover at most one such cell. Since there are N-1 such cells, we need at least N-1 rectangles to cover them. This lower bound does not require that each rectangle lies wholly in left region; it only uses the property that each rectangle can cover at most one c_i. That property holds even if rectangles cross between left and right region. So the lower bound for left region still holds: at least N-1 rectangles needed to cover all left region cells, because each rectangle can cover at most one of the c_i. However, we need to verify that covering all left region cells necessarily requires covering each c_i. Indeed, each left region cell is either a c_i (rightmost cell) or lies left of c_i in its row. So any rectangle covering left region cells must also cover the c_i for each row that has left region cells. Since each row with L_i>0 has at least one left region cell, that row's c_i must be covered by some rectangle. So we need at least N-1 rectangles. So the lower bound for left region holds even if some rectangles cross between left and right region.\n\nNow, does the lower bound for right region also hold? The argument is symmetric: define rightmost cells of right region? Actually, they define right region cells as those to the right of the hole. They use symmetry to argue similarly that at least N-1 rectangles needed to cover right region. The same reasoning can be applied: define for each row i with R_i>0 (i.e., p(i) < N) the leftmost cell of right region (i, p(i)+1). Those are distinct columns (since p(i) distinct). Then each rectangle can contain at most one such cell. So at least N-1 rectangles needed for right region. That holds.\n\nThus the lower bound of 2(N-1) holds even if some rectangles cross between left and right region. However, we need to verify that the argument for right region also holds: The right region cells are (i, p(i)+1) for each row i where p(i) < N. Since p(i) are a permutation of 1..N, there is exactly one row with p(i)=N, thus N-1 rows have p(i) < N. So there are N-1 such cells. They are distinct because p(i) distinct. For each such cell, any rectangle can contain at most one of them, by analogous reasoning: if a rectangle contains two such cells (i1, p(i1)+1) and (i2, p(i2)+1) with p(i1) < p(i2), then the rectangle's column interval must contain column p(i1)+1 and p(i2)+1, thus also column p(i1)+2, which is the hole (i1, p(i1)+1?) Wait, careful: The hole for row i1 is at column p(i1). The cell (i1, p(i1)+1) is the leftmost cell of right region. If rectangle contains both (i1, p(i1)+1) and (i2, p(i2)+1) with p(i1) < p(i2), then the rectangle's column interval J includes columns from p(i1)+1 to p(i2)+1, inclusive. That includes column p(i1)+1? Already included. Does it include column p(i1)? It might not. The interval from p(i1)+1 to p(i2)+1 does not include p(i1). So the rectangle does not necessarily contain the hole (i1, p(i1)). However, we need to consider the hole for row i2: p(i2). Since p(i2) > p(i1), and the interval includes p(i2)+1, it also includes p(i2) (since p(i2) is between p(i1)+1 and p(i2)+1). Indeed, if p(i2) >= p(i1)+1, then p(i2) is in the interval [p(i1)+1, p(i2)+1] (since p(i2) <= p(i2)+1). So the rectangle would contain column p(i2) for row i2, which is the hole for row i2. So any rectangle containing two distinct rightmost cells of right region would contain a hole. So each rectangle can contain at most one such cell. So the lower bound holds.\n\nThus the lower bound argument is valid.\n\nNow, we need to consider whether there could be a tiling using exactly N-1 tiles for left region and N-1 for right region, but perhaps some tiles could be used to cover both left and right region cells simultaneously, thereby reducing total number of tiles below 2(N-1). However, the lower bound argument counts tiles needed to cover left region cells (including c_i) and right region cells (including d_i). If a tile covers both a left region cell c_i and a right region cell d_j, does that reduce the count? The lower bound argument for left region says we need at least N-1 tiles to cover all left region cells, but it does not forbid a tile from also covering some right region cells. However, the argument for right region also says we need at least N-1 tiles to cover all right region cells. If a tile covers both a left region cell and a right region cell, it would be counted in both lower bounds. But the lower bound argument counts the total number of tiles used, not separate sets. If we have a tile that covers both left and right region cells, then that tile is counted once in the total, but it contributes to covering left region cells and right region cells. However, the lower bound argument for left region says at least N-1 tiles are needed to cover left region cells. That lower bound is based on the fact that each tile can cover at most one c_i. But a tile that also covers some right region cells still can only cover at most one c_i. So the lower bound for left region is still N-1 tiles, regardless of covering right region cells. Similarly, the lower bound for right region is N-1 tiles, each tile can cover at most one d_i. If a tile covers both a c_i and a d_j, then it counts as one tile in the total, but it counts as covering one c_i and one d_j. However, the lower bound for left region requires at least N-1 tiles, and the lower bound for right region also requires at least N-1 tiles. If we have a set of tiles that covers both left and right region cells, the total number of tiles must be at least max(N-1, N-1) = N-1? No, we need to consider that the same tile can be used to satisfy both lower bounds simultaneously. The lower bound for left region says: any tiling must have at least N-1 tiles. This is a lower bound on the total number of tiles, not just those covering left region. So any tiling must have at least N-1 tiles. Similarly, any tiling must have at least N-1 tiles (the same bound). So the total number of tiles must be at least N-1. However, we cannot combine the two lower bounds to get 2(N-1) unless we prove that the sets of tiles covering left region and right region are disjoint. The solution claims they are disjoint because each tile lies wholly in left region or wholly in right region. But we have found that this claim is false; there can be tiles that cross between left and right region. So the lower bounds for left and right region may not be additive. The total number of tiles might be as low as N-1, but we need to check if we can achieve that.\n\nThus the solution's lower bound of 2(N-1) might be invalid because it assumes disjointness. However, we need to examine if a tiling with fewer than 2(N-1) tiles is possible. Let's try to find a tiling with fewer tiles for N=2025 (or general N). Could we tile the complement of a permutation matrix with N tiles? Or maybe N-1 tiles? Let's explore.\n\nWe have N holes (one per row and column). The complement has N(N-1) cells. If we could tile it with N tiles, each tile would have average area (N(N-1))/N = N-1 cells per tile. That's plausible. The solution uses 2(N-1) tiles, each tile average area (N(N-1))/(2(N-1)) = N/2 cells per tile. So maybe we can do better.\n\nConsider the identity permutation. The complement consists of two triangles. Could we tile the whole complement with N tiles? For example, we could tile each row i (i > 1) with a horizontal rectangle covering all cells in that row except the hole at column i. That would be a rectangle of length N-1 (covering columns 1..i-1 and i+1..N). But that rectangle is not contiguous because it would have a hole in the middle. So not allowed. So we need to split each row into two rectangles: left part (columns 1..i-1) and right part (columns i+1..N). So each row would need at least 2 rectangles unless one of the parts is empty (i.e., i=1 or i=N). So we need at least 2(N-2) + 2 = 2N-2 rectangles? Actually, for i=1, left part empty, right part columns 2..N (N-1 cells) can be covered by a single rectangle. For i=N, right part empty, left part columns 1..N-1 (N-1 cells) can be covered by a single rectangle. For rows i=2..N-1, both left and right parts non-empty, requiring at least 2 rectangles per row? However, we could combine left parts of multiple rows into a single rectangle that spans multiple rows, as the solution does. So the lower bound per row is not needed.\n\nAlternatively, we could use rectangles that cover many rows and many columns, but must avoid holes. The holes are on the diagonal. So any rectangle that includes rows a..b and columns c..d must avoid the diagonal cells (i,i) for i in [a,b] ∩ [c,d]. So to avoid holes, we need either c > b (rectangle entirely to the right of diagonal for all rows) or d < a (rectangle entirely to the left of diagonal for all rows). This is the condition we derived earlier: J ∩ I = ∅. So any rectangle must be either entirely left of the diagonal (i.e., columns less than the smallest row index) or entirely right of the diagonal (columns greater than the largest row index). This is true for identity permutation (p(i)=i). For a general permutation, the condition is J ∩ {p(i) : i ∈ I} = ∅. But p(i) is a permutation, not necessarily monotonic. So J must avoid the set of columns p(i) for rows i in I. Since p(i) are distinct but not necessarily contiguous, J could be an interval that lies between some of the p(i) values, as long as it doesn't intersect any of them. For example, if p(i) values for rows in I are {2,5,7}, then J could be [3,4] (which avoids those values). That interval is not necessarily left of all rows or right of all rows; it's between some p(i) values. So the rectangle can be placed in a \"gap\" between holes in the permutation. That rectangle would lie left of some holes and right of others, but not intersect any hole. So indeed, for a general permutation, rectangles can cross between left and right region relative to each row's hole.\n\nThus the solution's claim that each tile lies wholly in L or R is false for a general permutation. However, the lower bound argument for left region used the rightmost cells of left region (c_i) which are distinct columns. The argument that each rectangle can contain at most one c_i holds for any permutation because c_i = (i, p(i)-1). If a rectangle contains two such cells (i1, p(i1)-1) and (i2, p(i2)-1) with p(i1) < p(i2)