a d_j. So the lower bound stands.\n\nThus the solution's lower bound is valid, albeit the justification that each tile lies wholly in L or R is unnecessary but not harmful. The proof of lower bound uses Lemma 1 about c_i and then symmetry for right region. The argument about symmetry presumably uses the same reasoning for d_i. So the lower bound is correct.\n\nNow, we need to verify if the solution indeed proves that a rectangle cannot contain both a c_i and a d_j. The solution does not explicitly state that, but they claim that any tile lies wholly in L or wholly in R, which implies that a tile cannot contain both a left region cell and a right region cell. However, we found that claim is false. But they used it to argue that the tiling splits into left region tiling and right region tiling. If that claim is false, then the additive lower bound argument is not justified. However, they also gave a direct lower bound for left region (N-1) and then for right region (N-1) via symmetry and then added them. The symmetry argument presumably uses the same claim that any tile lies wholly in left region or wholly in right region, which is false. So the additive lower bound may be invalid.\n\nBut we need to examine if the additive lower bound can be derived without that claim. The solution's reasoning for left region lower bound is correct. For right region, they say \"Apply a horizontal reflection (replace column j by N+1-j). Under this symmetry \\mathcal R is transformed into a left region of the same type, and the argument of Section 4 applies verbatim.\" However, the reflection also transforms the tile set: a tile that crosses between left and right region in the original configuration might become a tile that also crosses between left and right region in the reflected configuration. The argument of Section 4 applied to the reflected configuration would give a lower bound of N-1 for the number of tiles needed to cover the reflected right region. But that lower bound counts tiles in the reflected configuration, which correspond to the same tiles in the original configuration (since reflection is a bijection). However, the argument of Section 4 for the reflected configuration also uses the claim that each tile lies wholly in left region (in the reflected configuration). That claim is also false. So the lower bound for right region may also be invalid.\n\nThus the solution's lower bound may be flawed because it incorrectly assumes that each tile lies wholly in left or right region. However, we need to check if the lower bound can be salvaged: perhaps the claim that each tile lies wholly in left region or wholly in right region is actually true for any permutation? Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe have a permutation p: {1,...,N} -> {1,...,N}. For a tile I x J, we need that for each i ∈ I, J does not contain p(i). So J must avoid the set {p(i) : i ∈ I}. Since J is an interval, it must be a subinterval of the complement of that set within {1,...,N}. The complement of a set of points in an interval is a union of intervals. So J must be contained in one of those intervals. Those intervals are the gaps between the p(i) values for i ∈ I. So J can be placed in a gap between p(i) values. If the p(i) values are not monotonic, there may be multiple gaps. So J can be placed in any gap. So J could be to the left of all p(i) values (i.e., J ⊆ [1, min(p(i))-1]), or to the right of all p(i) values (J ⊆ [max(p(i))+1, N]), or in a gap between some p(i) values. So J could be \"between\" some p(i) values. In that case, for some rows i, p(i) > J (hole to the right), for others p(i) < J (hole to the left). So the rectangle would be left of some holes and right of others. So the claim that each tile lies wholly in L or wholly in R is false for a general permutation.\n\nThus the solution's claim is false. However, does the lower bound rely on that claim? Let's parse the solution:\n\n- Section 3: \"Every tile lies completely in L or in R\". They prove it us