n (holes on diagonal). Then left region consists of cells below diagonal (i > j). They cover each column c (1 <= c <= N-1) with a vertical tile covering rows i = c+1 to N (so a tall vertical rectangle of width 1). That's N-c rows. So each column left of diagonal is covered by one tile. That's N-1 tiles. Similarly right region (above diagonal) is covered by vertical tiles for each column c (2 <= c <= N) covering rows i = 1 to c-1. That's c-1 rows. So N-1 tiles. So total 2(N-1) tiles.\n\nThus answer 4048.\n\nWe need to verify lower bound correctness: Are there any ways to use fewer than N-1 tiles for left region? The solution's argument uses the fact that L_i = p(i)-1 are distinct values from 0 to N-1. So there are N-1 rows with L_i > 0. For each such row, they consider the rightmost cell of left region in that row: (i, L_i). They claim no rectangle can contain two distinct such cells because if it contains (i, L_i) and (i', L_i') with L_i < L_i', then the rectangle's column interval includes columns L_i and L_i', thus also includes column L_i+1, which in row i is the hole (i, p(i)). Indeed, p(i) = L_i+1. So the rectangle would contain a hole, which is not allowed. So each rectangle can contain at most one of those cells. Since there are N-1 such cells, need at least N-1 rectangles. That seems correct.\n\nPotential issues: Does this argument assume that each rectangle is axis-aligned? Yes, the tiles are rectangular with sides on grid lines, so axis-aligned. The rectangle's column interval is contiguous. So the argument holds.\n\nSimilarly for right region.\n\nThus lower bound is correct.\n\nConstruction: The construction uses vertical tiles of width 1 covering each column left of diagonal (below diagonal) and each column right of diagonal (above diagonal). Does each tile lie entirely in left region or right region? Yes. Does it cover all cells left of diagonal? For column c, rows i = c+1,...,N. Those are exactly the cells with column c and row > column, i.e., left region cells where column index less than row index. Indeed left region = cells left of diagonal: for each row i, columns j < i. So for each column c, the cells (i,c) with i > c are left region cells. So vertical tile covers all left region cells in column c. So left region is covered by N-1 vertical tiles. Similarly right region: for column c (2 <= c <= N), rows i = 1,...,c-1 (i < c) are right region cells (above diagonal). So vertical tile covers all right region cells in column c. So right region covered by N-1 vertical tiles. So total 2(N-1) tiles. Works.\n\nThus answer is correct and reasoning is valid.\n\nNow we need to check if any presentation issues: The solution is fairly thorough. It includes a lemma and a proof. It uses definitions and arguments. It might have some minor gaps: For example, the claim \"If there exist rows i1,i2 in I for which (1) holds for i1 and (2) holds for i2, then the column interval [c,d] would contain both a column < p(i1) and a column > p(i2), which is impossible because [c,d] is contiguous.\" Actually, it's not impossible: a contiguous interval can contain both a column less than p(i1) and a column greater than p(i2) if p(i1) < p(i2). But the argument is that the interval would contain p(i1) and p(i2) maybe? Let's examine: Suppose we have a rectangle I x J, with rows i1 and i2. For row i1, the rectangle lies left of the hole: d < p(i1). For row i2, rectangle lies right of the hole: c > p(i2). So we have d < p(i1) and c > p(i2). Since c <= d (since J = [c,d]), we get c <= d. But c > p(i2) and d < p(i1). So we need p(i2) < c <= d < p(i1). So p(i2) < p(i1). So the interval [c,d] lies between p(i2) and p(i1). That's possible if p(i2) < p(i1). But the rectangle's column interval being contiguous does not preclude that. Actually, the rectangle's column interval [c,d] must be a contiguous set of columns. The condition d < p(i1) and c > p(i2) can be satisfied if p(i2) < p(i1) and there is a gap between p(i2) and p(i1). But the rectangle's column interval would be a set of columns that are all less than p(i1) and all greater than p(i2). That is possible only if p(i2) < p(i1) and there are columns between them. But the rectangle's column interval must be contiguous, so it can be any interval [c,d] with c <= d. The condition c > p(i2) and d < p(i1) yields c > p(i2) and d < p(i1). Since c <= d, we need p(i2) < p(i1) and there exists an integer interval (c,d) that lies between them. That's possible if p(i2)+1 <= p(i1)-1. So the rectangle could be placed entirely between the two holes. However, the rectangle also includes rows i1 and i2. For row i1, the hole is at column p(i1). The rectangle's columns are all less than p(i1) (since d < p(i1)), so it's left of the hole for row i1. For row i2, rectangle's columns are all greater than p(i2) (since c > p(i2)), so it's right of the hole for row i2. So the rectangle straddles the holes: left of hole in one row, right of hole in another row. The solution claims this is impossible because the column interval is contiguous. But as we just reasoned, it's possible if the holes are at different columns and the rectangle's column interval lies between them. However, does that rectangle contain any hole? Let's examine: For row i1, the hole is at column p(i1). Since rectangle's columns are all less than p(i1), it does not contain the hole. For row i2, rectangle's columns are all greater than p(i2), so does not contain the hole. So the rectangle does not contain any hole. So the argument that such a rectangle cannot exist might be flawed. But the solution's later argument uses the fact that each tile must be wholly on one side of all holes in its rows. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe need to verify the claim: For a rectangle I x J, if there exist rows i1,i2 in I such that for i1 the rectangle lies left of the hole (i.e., d < p(i1)), and for i2 the rectangle lies right of the hole (c > p(i2)), then that rectangle cannot exist because J is contiguous. Actually, we need to consider the condition that for each row i in I, the rectangle cannot intersect the hole at column p(i). So for each row i, either all columns of J are less than p(i) (i.e., J lies left of the hole) or all columns of J are greater than p(i) (i.e., J lies right of the hole). The rectangle's column interval J is fixed across all rows. So if for some rows the hole is to the right of J (i.e., J left of hole) and for other rows the hole is to the left of J (i.e., J right of hole), then the holes must be on both sides of J. That is possible if the holes' columns vary across rows. For example, suppose we have two rows: row 1 hole at column 5, row 2 hole at column 2. If we take J = [3,4], then for row 1, J = [3,4] is left of hole at column 5 (since d=4 < p(1)=5). For row 2, J = [3,4] is right of hole at column 2 (since c=3 > p(2)=2). So J lies between the holes. This rectangle does not contain any hole. So it's allowed. So the claim that such a rectangle cannot exist is false. However, the solution's later argument about left region and right region uses the fact that each tile lies entirely in left region or right region. But we just found a possible tile that straddles holes: a rectangle that lies between holes in different rows. Does that tile lie entirely in left region or right region? Let's examine left region L = { (i,j) : j < p(i) }. Right region R = { (i,j) : j > p(i) }. The rectangle we described includes cells (i, j) where for row 1, j = 3,4 < p(1) = 5 => those cells are in left region for row 1. For row 2, j = 3,4 > p(2) = 2 => those cells are in right region for row 2. So the rectangle contains cells that are in left region for some rows and right region for other rows. So it's not wholly in left region or right region. So the solution's claim that any tile must be wholly in left region or right region is false. However, does that affect the lower bound? The lower bound argument used the fact that each tile lies wholly in left region or right region to separate the tiling into two independent tilings. If there exist tiles that cross between left and right region (i.e., contain cells on both sides of holes across rows), then the lower bound argument might be invalid because you could potentially use fewer tiles by using such crossing tiles. The solution's lower bound argument might be flawed.\n\nWe need to examine if such crossing tiles are possible given the condition that each row and column has exactly one uncovered square. The holes are placed at positions (i, p(i)). The left region is cells left of each hole, right region is cells right of each hole. A tile that straddles holes across rows would have some cells left of the hole in some rows and right of the hole in other rows. Is that allowed? Yes, as long as the tile does not cover any hole. The tile's column interval J must avoid all holes in rows I. That is, for each i in I, either J is entirely left of p(i) or entirely right of p(i). But J can be left of p(i) for some rows and right of p(i) for others, as long as J does not intersect any hole. So the tile can be \"between\" holes across rows.\n\nThus the claim that any tile must be wholly in L or wholly in R is false. However, the solution's argument for that claim may be flawed. Let's examine the exact wording:\n\n> Let a tile be an axis‑parallel rectangle I×J, where I=[a,b]⊂{1,…,N}, J=[c,d]⊂{1,…,N}. For a fixed row i∈I the uncovered cell (hole) lies at column p(i). Since the rectangle does not contain that hole, either (1) d<p(i) (the rectangle lies left of the hole in row i) or (2) c>p(i) (the rectangle lies right of the hole in row i). If there exist rows i1,i2∈I for which (1) holds for i1 and (2) holds for i2, then the column interval [c,d] would contain both a column <p(i1) and a column >p(i2), which is impossible because [c,d] is contiguous. Hence the same inequality holds for all rows i∈I.\n\nThe reasoning is flawed: The interval [c,d] being contiguous does not prevent it from containing both a column less than p(i1) and a column greater than p(i2). It can contain columns that are less than p(i1) and greater than p(i2) if p(i2) < p(i1) and the interval lies between them. The condition \"contains both a column < p(i1) and a column > p(i2)\" is not contradictory. The interval can contain both types of columns. The only way it would be impossible is if p(i1) <= p(i2), then having a column less than p(i1) and a column greater than p(i2) would require the interval to span across p(i1) and p(i2) and thus contain p(i1) or p(i2). But the solution didn't consider ordering of p(i1) and p(i2). The argument is insufficient.\n\nThus the claim that any tile lies wholly in left region or right region is not proven. Indeed, there exist possible tiles that cross between left and right region across rows. However, does the existence of such crossing tiles allow a tiling with fewer than 2(N-1) tiles? Possibly. Let's explore if we can find a tiling with fewer tiles using crossing tiles.\n\nWe need to consider the problem: we have N holes (one per row and column). We need to tile the rest of the board with axis-aligned rectangles covering disjoint cells. We want to minimize number of rectangles. The solution claims the minimum is 2(N-1). But is that indeed minimal? Let's try to think about possible tilings that use fewer rectangles.\n\nWe need to cover all cells except the holes. The holes form a permutation matrix. The board is N x N. The total number of cells to cover is N^2 - N = N(N-1). Each rectangle covers some number of cells. The goal is to minimize number of rectangles, i.e., maximize total area per rectangle. However, there are constraints due to holes: rectangles cannot include holes. So they must be placed in the complement of the holes.\n\nOne might think that the complement of a permutation matrix can be tiled by N-1 rectangles: For example, consider the identity permutation. The complement consists of all cells not on the diagonal. This is the union of two triangular regions: lower triangle (i > j) and upper triangle (i < j). The solution uses vertical strips (columns) to cover each triangle separately, requiring N-1 rectangles per triangle, total 2(N-1). But perhaps we can tile each triangle with fewer rectangles using larger rectangles that cross rows and columns. For example, consider the lower triangle: we could tile it with a single rectangle covering rows 2..N and columns 1..N-1? That rectangle would include cells (i,j) where i >= 2 and j <= N-1. But does it include any hole? The holes are at (i,i) for i=1..N. The rectangle includes (i,i) for i from 2..N-1? Let's check: For i=2, j runs from 1..N-1, includes j=2, which is a hole (2,2). So that rectangle would contain a hole. So not allowed.\n\nBut perhaps we can tile the lower triangle with N-2 rectangles? Let's think: The lower triangle is the set of cells (i,j) with i > j. This region is a Young diagram shape. It can be tiled by rectangles of shape (i-j) x 1? Actually, the solution uses vertical strips of width 1 for each column. Could we use horizontal strips of height 1 for each row? That would also be N-1 rectangles. Could we combine some columns? For example, can we tile the lower triangle with a rectangle covering rows i = 3..N and columns j = 1..2? That rectangle includes cells (i,j) where i>=3, j<=2. Does it contain any hole? The holes are at (i,i). For i >= 3, j <= 2, we have j != i, so no hole. So that rectangle is allowed. So we could combine columns 1 and 2 into a rectangle covering rows 3..N. But we need to also cover cells (2,1) and (3,2)? Let's check: The lower triangle includes cells: (2,1), (3,1), (3,2), (4,1), (4,2), (4,3), etc. If we take rectangle covering rows 3..N and columns 1..2, that covers all cells (i,j) with i>=3, j=1 or 2. That includes (3,1), (3,2), (4,1), (4,2), (5,1),(5,2),..., (N,1),(N,2). It does not cover (2,1). So we still need to cover (2,1). That could be covered by a rectangle covering row 2, column 1 (a 1x1 tile). So we would have 1 tile for column 1 rows 3..N (height N-2), 1 tile for column 2 rows 3..N (height N-2), plus 1 tile for (2,1). That's 3 tiles for covering columns 1 and 2. The solution uses 2 tiles for column 1 (vertical tile covering rows 2..N) and column 2 (vertical tile covering rows 3..N). Actually the solution uses for each column c a vertical tile covering rows i=c+1..N. So for column 1: rows 2..N (height N-1). For column 2: rows 3..N (height N-2). So they already combine rows for each column individually. So the solution uses N-1 tiles for left region. Could we reduce number of tiles by merging columns? For example, could we tile columns 1 and 2 together in a rectangle covering rows 3..N and columns 1..2? That is a rectangle of size (N-2) x 2. This rectangle does not contain any hole because holes are at (i,i) with i >= 3, but column indices are 1 or 2, so i != j. So it's allowed. So we can combine columns 1 and 2 into one rectangle. Then we need to cover (2,1) and (2,2?) Actually (2,2) is a hole, not needed. So we need to cover (2,1) only. That could be a 1x1 tile. So we would have 1 tile for (2,1) plus 1 tile covering rows 3..N, columns 1..2. That's 2 tiles for covering left region cells in columns 1 and 2, whereas the solution uses 2 tiles (col1: rows 2..N, col2: rows 3..N). So same number. But maybe we can combine more columns.\n\nConsider merging columns 1,2,3 into a rectangle covering rows 4..N and columns 1..3. That rectangle covers all cells (i,j) with i>=4, j<=3. Does it contain any hole? For i>=4, j<=3, i != j, so no hole. So allowed. Then we need to cover cells in rows 2,3 for columns <=3. Let's list left region cells for columns 1..3:\n\n- Column 1: rows 2..N (i>1)\n- Column 2: rows 3..N (i>2)\n- Column 3: rows 4..N (i>3)\n\nIf we take rectangle covering rows 4..N, columns 1..3, that covers:\n\n- For column 1: rows 4..N (i>=4)\n- For column 2: rows 4..N (i>=4)\n- For column 3: rows 4..N (i>=4)\n\nThus we still need to cover:\n\n- Column 1: rows 2,3 (i=2,3)\n- Column 2: rows 3 (i=3)\n- Column 3: none (since rows 4..N already covered, and row 3 column 3 is a hole? Actually (3,3) is a hole, not needed.)\n\nSo we need to cover cells: (2,1), (3,1), (3,2). That's three cells. They could be covered by a rectangle covering rows 2..3, columns 1..2 (size 2x2). Does that rectangle contain any hole? It includes (2,2) which is a hole (since identity permutation). So not allowed. So we cannot combine those three cells into a rectangle without covering a hole. So we need separate tiles: maybe (2,1) as 1x1, (3,1) as 1x1, (3,2) as 1x1, or combine (2,1) and (3,1) into a vertical tile covering rows 2..3, column 1 (size 2x1). That rectangle includes (2,1) and (3,1). Does it contain any hole? It does not include (2,2) or (3,3), so it's allowed. So we can have tile covering (2,1),(3,1). Then we need (3,2) as a 1x1 tile. So total tiles for columns 1..3: rectangle covering rows 4..N, columns 1..3 (1 tile), vertical tile covering rows 2..3, column 1 (1 tile), 1x1 tile covering (3,2) (1 tile). That's 3 tiles. The solution uses 3 tiles for columns 1,2,3: column1: rows 2..N (1 tile), column2: rows 3..N (1 tile), column3: rows 4..N (1 tile). That's also 3 tiles. So no improvement.\n\nBut perhaps we can reduce number of tiles by using larger rectangles that cover many rows and columns simultaneously, but we must avoid holes. The holes are on the diagonal. So any rectangle that includes both a row i and a column j such that i=j would contain a hole if the rectangle includes the cell (i,i). However, if the rectangle's column interval does not include the column index equal to the row index for any row in its row interval, then it's okay. That is, for rectangle I = [a,b] rows, J = [c,d] columns, we need for each i in [a,b] that either d < i (i.e., J entirely left of i) or c > i (J entirely right of i). So the rectangle must be either entirely left of the diagonal for all rows in its row interval, or entirely right of the diagonal for all rows. However, the rectangle could be left of the diagonal for some rows and right of the diagonal for others, as long as the column interval J lies between the smallest and largest row indices? Actually, the condition is: For each i in I, either J is left of i (i.e., d < i) or J is right of i (c > i). The rectangle can have J left of i for some i and right of i for others. But for a given rectangle, J is fixed. So we need to find I and J such that for each i in I, either d < i or c > i. This is possible if J is a contiguous interval that does not intersect the set {i: i in I} (the set of row indices). But the condition is not about intersecting the set of row indices; it's about intersecting the diagonal cells (i,i). The rectangle cannot contain any cell (i,i). That means we need to avoid the diagonal cells. For each i in I, we need to avoid column i. So J must not contain i. So J must be a subset of {1,...,i-1} ∪ {i+1,...,N} for each i. So J cannot contain any i in I. So J must be a subset of columns that avoid all row indices in I. That is, J ∩ I = ∅. So J must be a set of columns that does not intersect the set of rows I. Since J is an interval, we need an interval that does not intersect I. So either J lies entirely to the left of min(I) (i.e., d < min(I)), or J lies entirely to the right of max(I) (i.e., c > max(I)). Because if J is an interval and I is also an interval (since rows are contiguous), then J cannot be partially left of some rows and partially right of others without intersecting some row index. Let's examine: I = [a,b] (contiguous rows). J = [c,d] (contiguous columns). Condition J ∩ I = ∅. Since both intervals are contiguous, the only way they can be disjoint is either d < a (J left of I) or c > b (J right of I). If J is between a and b (i.e., a <= c <= d <= b), then J intersects I. If J overlaps partially, e.g., c < a <= d < b, then J intersects I because d >= a. So the only way for J and I to be disjoint intervals is J entirely left of I or J entirely right of I. So the rectangle must be wholly left of the diagonal (i.e., columns less than all rows in I) or wholly right of the diagonal (columns greater than all rows in I). This is a stronger condition than the earlier analysis. Let's verify: The condition J ∩ I = ∅ is necessary because for each i in I, we need i ∉ J. So J cannot contain any integer i that is also in I. Since I is an interval of integers, J must avoid all those integers. Since J is also an interval, it cannot \"skip\" over them; it must be either entirely to the left or entirely to the right. So indeed, any rectangle cannot have J left of some rows and right of others; it must be wholly left of all rows or wholly right of all rows. However, earlier we gave an example where I = {1,2} (rows 1 and 2) and J = [3,4] (columns 3 and 4). Here I = [1,2], J = [3,4]. J is to the right of I (c=3 > b=2). So J is wholly right of I. That matches the condition. Our earlier example with I = {1,2} and J = [3,4] is indeed wholly right of I. That is consistent with the condition. The earlier example with I = {1,2} and J = [3,4] is okay. But we gave an example where J = [3,4] and holes at p(1)=5, p(2)=2. Wait, p(2)=2, so row 2 hole is at column 2. J = [3,4] is to the right of row 2 (c=3 > 2). For row 1, hole at column 5, J = [3,4] is left of hole (d=4 < 5). So J is left of hole for row 1 and right of hole for row 2. But J is still to the right of row 2 (since row 2 index = 2). So J is to the right of all rows in I? Actually, I = {1,2}. The maximum row index is 2. J = [3,4] has c=3 > 2, so J is to the right of all rows. So J is wholly right of I. So the rectangle is wholly right of the diagonal for all rows in I (since the diagonal cells are at (i,i), and J's columns are > i for each i in I). Indeed, for row 1, J's columns are 3,4, which are > 1, so J is right of the diagonal for row 1. For row 2, J's columns are 3,4, which are > 2, so J is right of the diagonal for row 2. So the rectangle lies entirely in the right region for all rows. So the earlier claim that the rectangle is left of hole for row 1 is not correct: The hole for row 1 is at column 5, but J = [3,4] is left of 5, but still J is right of row index 1, so it's still in the right region (since right region is defined as columns > p(i) or columns > i? Wait, right region is defined as columns > p(i). Actually, the right region is defined as columns > p(i), not > i. So for row 1, p(1) = 5, J = [3,4] is not > p(1). So J is not in right region for row 1. But the earlier argument about region L and R is based on p(i), not i. So the rectangle is left of hole for row 1, right of hole for row 2. So the rectangle is not wholly in left region (since for row 2 it's right of hole) nor wholly in right region (since for row 1 it's left of hole). However, does that contradict the condition J ∩ I = ∅? Let's check: I = {1,2}, J = {3,4}. I = {1,2}, J = {3,4}. They are disjoint intervals. So J ∩ I = ∅. So the rectangle does not contain any hole (i,i). But does it contain any hole (i,p(i))? The holes are at (1,5) and (2,2). The rectangle includes columns 3,4, rows 1,2. So it does not include (1,5) because column 5 not in J. It does not include (2,2) because column 2 not in J. So it's allowed. However, the rectangle includes cells (1,3), (1,4), (2,3), (2,4). For row 1, those cells are left of the hole (since hole at column 5). For row 2, those cells are right of the hole (hole at column 2). So the rectangle straddles left and right region across rows. So the earlier claim that any tile must be wholly in left region or wholly in right region is false. However, the condition J ∩ I = ∅ ensures that the rectangle does not intersect the diagonal (i,i). But the region L and R are defined relative to p(i), not i. So the rectangle can straddle left and right region relative to p(i) if p(i) are not monotonic with i.\n\nThus the solution's claim that any tile lies wholly in L or wholly in R is false. However, they later use this claim to argue that the tiling splits into left region tiling and right region tiling. Since it's false, the lower bound may not hold. But perhaps the lower bound still holds even if some tiles cross between L and R. We need to examine whether crossing tiles could reduce the number of tiles needed below 2(N-1). The lower bound argument uses the fact that each tile can cover at most one of the rightmost cells in left region (c_i). If a tile can cross between left and right region, could it cover two such rightmost cells? The rightmost cells c_i are defined as (i, L_i) where L_i = p(i)-1. These cells are the rightmost cells of left region in each row. If a tile crosses between left and right region, can it contain two such cells? Let's examine.\n\nSuppose we have two rows i1,i2 with L_i1 < L_i2 (since L_i are distinct). The cell c_i1 = (i1, L_i1) is the rightmost cell left of hole in row i1. The cell c_i2 = (i2, L_i2) is the rightmost cell left of hole in row i2. If a rectangle includes both cells, then its column interval J must contain both columns L_i1 and L_i2. Since L_i1 < L_i2, J must contain all columns between them, including L_i1+1. For row i1, column L_i1+1 is p(i1) (hole). So the rectangle would contain a hole, which is not allowed. So indeed, any rectangle cannot contain two distinct rightmost cells c_i. This holds regardless of whether the rectangle also contains cells on the right side of holes for other rows. Because the argument only uses the fact that the rectangle contains both columns L_i1 and L_i2, which implies it contains column L_i1+1, which is the hole for row i1. So any rectangle cannot contain two distinct rightmost cells c_i. So each rectangle can cover at most one such cell. Since there are N-1 such cells, we need at least N-1 rectangles to cover them. This lower bound does not require that each rectangle lies wholly in left region; it only uses the property that each rectangle can cover at most one c_i. That property holds even if rectangles cross between left and right region. So the lower bound for left region still holds: at least N-1 rectangles needed to cover all left region cells, because each rectangle can cover at most one of the c_i. However, we need to verify that covering all left region cells necessarily requires covering each c_i. Indeed, each left region cell is either a c_i (rightmost cell) or lies left of c_i in its row. So any rectangle covering left region cells must also cover the c_i for each row that has left region cells. Since each row with L_i>0 has at least one left region cell, that row's c_i must be covered by some rectangle. So we need at least N-1 rectangles. So the lower bound for left region holds even if some rectangles cross between left and right region.\n\nNow, does the lower bound for right region also hold? The argument is symmetric: define rightmost cells of right region? Actually, they define right region cells as those to the right of the hole. They use symmetry to argue similarly that at least N-1 rectangles needed to cover right region. The same reasoning can be applied: define for each row i with R_i>0 (i.e., p(i) < N) the leftmost cell of right region (i, p(i)+1). Those are distinct columns (since p(i) distinct). Then each rectangle can contain at most one such cell. So at least N-1 rectangles needed for right region. That holds.\n\nThus the lower bound of 2(N-1) holds even if some rectangles cross between left and right region. However, we need to verify that the argument for right region also holds: The right region cells are (i, p(i)+1) for each row i where p(i) < N. Since p(i) are a permutation of 1..N, there is exactly one row with p(i)=N, thus N-1 rows have p(i) < N. So there are N-1 such cells. They are distinct because p(i) distinct. For each such cell, any rectangle can contain at most one of them, by analogous reasoning: if a rectangle contains two such cells (i1, p(i1)+1) and (i2, p(i2)+1) with p(i1) < p(i2), then the rectangle's column interval must contain column p(i1)+1 and p(i