e rows it covers, or entirely to the right of all holes for those rows. But the solution's argument says a rectangle cannot contain cells from both L and R. Let's examine: L is union of all cells left of the hole in each row; R is union of all cells right of the hole in each row. Indeed any rectangle that includes a cell left of a hole in some row and a cell right of a hole in some (maybe same or different) row would have to cross the hole column for that row, which would include the hole cell. So indeed any rectangle must be wholly within L or wholly within R. That seems correct.\n\nThus we can treat covering L and covering R separately.\n\nNow, they argue that covering L requires at least n-1 rectangles. They define a_i = π(i)-1 (the number of cells left of hole in row i). Since π is a permutation of {1,...,n}, the multiset {a_i} = {0,1,...,n-1}. So there is exactly one row with a_i = k for each k from 0 to n-1. They then argue that each rectangle in L has a rightmost column c, and that c must equal some a_i for some row i in the rectangle. So each rectangle's rightmost column corresponds to a value k, and each k must appear as rightmost column of some rectangle covering L. Since there are n-1 possible non-zero k (1,...,n-1) (k=0 corresponds to no left cells?), they claim at least n-1 rectangles needed.\n\nWe need to verify the argument: For each rectangle Q ⊆ L, define its column interval J = [j1, j2] (inclusive). Since Q ⊆ L, for each row i in I (the row interval of Q), we have J ⊆ {1,...,a_i} (i.e., columns up to a_i). So the rightmost column c = max J ≤ a_i for each i ∈ I, so c ≤ min_i a_i. So c ≤ min_i a_i. Then they claim if c = k, then there must be a row i in I with a_i = k (i.e., the minimum equals k). Indeed if c = k, then min_i a_i ≥ k (since c ≤ min_i a_i). But also c is the maximum column used, and each row i must have a_i ≥ c (since J ⊆ {1,...,a_i}). So a_i ≥ c for all rows i. So min_i a_i = some value ≥ c. But can min_i a_i be > c? Suppose all rows 