vered unit square, call it the *hole* of row \\(i\\).  \nSince each column also contains exactly one hole, the set of holes forms a permutation matrix:\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\n\\text{hole in row }i\\text{ is at column }\\sigma(i).\n\\]\n\nA *tile* is an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; tiles may not overlap and must avoid all holes.  \nWe must cover every non\u2011hole square by exactly one tile.  \nThe problem asks for the minimum possible number of tiles over all choices of the permutation \\(\\sigma\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound \u2013 a universal construction\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) let \\(r_j=\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\) be the row of the hole in that column.  \nConsider the two (possibly empty) vertical intervals\n\n\\[\nU_j=\\{(i,j):1\\le i< r_j\\},\\qquad\nD_j=\\{(i,j):r_j< i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(U_j\\) and \\(D_j\\) are contiguous sets of unit squares; each is a single rectangle.\nIf \\(r_j=1\\) then \\(U_j\\) is empty, and if \\(r_j=n\\) then \\(D_j\\) is empty.\n\nPlace a tile on every non\u2011empty set \\(U_j\\) and on every non\u2011empty set \\(D_j\\).  \nThese tiles are pairwise disjoint (they lie in distinct columns) and together cover every square that is not a hole.  \n\nCounting the tiles:  \n*Exactly two columns contain the holes in the top row (\\(r_j=1\\)) and the bottom row (\\(r_j=n\\)); each of those columns contributes one tile.*  \nAll remaining \\(n-2\\) columns contribute two tiles each (one above, one below the hole).  \nHence the total number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n2+(n-2)\\cdot2 = 2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this yields \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound \u2013 critical cells\n\nWe show that **any** tiling must use at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n---\n\n##### 2.1  Cells left of the holes\n\nDefine for each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\)\n\n\\[\ni_k = \\sigma^{-1}(k+1) \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nc_k = (i_k,\\,k).\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\(c_k\\) lies in row \\(i_k\\) and column \\(k\\); because \\(\\sigma(i_k)=k+1\\), column \\(k\\) is strictly left of the hole in that row, so \\(c_k\\) belongs to the region **left** of all holes.\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal C=\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_{\\,n-1}\\}\\) contains exactly one cell in each column \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\) and in distinct rows (the rows \\(i_1,\\dots ,i_{\\,n-1}\\) are all different because \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation).\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Claim.}\\quad\\text{No axis\u2011parallel rectangle contained entirely in the left region can contain two distinct cells of }\\mathcal C.\n\\]\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\) (with \\(I,J\\) intervals of rows and columns) lies completely left of every hole and contains two cells \\(c_a=(i_a,a)\\) and \\(c_b=(i_b,b)\\) with \\(a<b\\).  \nSince both columns \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) belong to \\(J\\), the whole interval \\([a,b]\\) is contained in \\(J\\).  \nBut \\(\\sigma(i_a)=a+1\\); consequently column \\(a+1\\) also belongs to \\(J\\).  \nBecause \\(i_a\\in I\\), the rectangle \\(R\\) would contain the cell \\((i_a,a+1)\\), which is precisely the hole in row \\(i_a\\) \u2013 a contradiction. \u220e\n\nThus each rectangle covering the left region can contain **at most one** cell of \\(\\mathcal C\\).  \nSince all cells of the left region must be covered, at least \\(|\\mathcal C|=n-1\\) rectangles are necessary for that region.\n\n---\n\n##### 2.2  Cells right of the holes\n\nA symmetric argument works on the right side.  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\ni'_k = \\sigma^{-1}(n-k),\\qquad\nc'_k = (i'_k,\\,n-k+1).\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\(c'_k\\) lies strictly **right** of the hole in its row.  \nExactly the same reasoning shows that any rectangle lying wholly to the right of all holes can contain at most one of the cells \\(c'_1,\\dots ,c'_{\\,n