se rows are at columns < k). In those rows, the tile would be to the right of the hole. But the tile's column interval J is contiguous and includes column k. If there is any row i' in I such that its hole column σ(i') < k, then the tile would be to the right of that hole. But the tile may still be allowed because the tile does not contain that hole (since hole column is left of J). So the tile could be both left of some holes and right of others. The solution's claim \"any tile that covers a cell of L must be a left-rectangle\" means that the tile is left of the holes in all its rows. But is that necessarily true? Let's examine.\n\nDefinition: A left-rectangle is defined as a rectangle such that for every row i in I, max J < σ(i). That is, the rectangle lies left of the hole in each of its rows. Similarly, a right-rectangle is one where min J > σ(i) for all i in I.\n\nNow suppose a tile covers a cell c_k = (σ^{-1}(k+1), k). The tile's column interval J includes column k. For the tile to avoid covering the hole at (σ^{-1}(k+1), k+1), J must not include column k+1. So max J ≤ k. So for the row i0 = σ^{-1}(k+1), we have max J ≤ k < σ(i0) = k+1. So for that row, the rectangle is left of the hole. However, for other rows i in I, we need to check if the rectangle is left of the hole in those rows as well. Could there be a row i' where σ(i') ≤ k? That would mean the hole in that row is at column ≤ k. Then max J ≤ k may be ≥ σ(i')? Actually if σ(i') ≤ k, then max J ≤ k may be >= σ(i'), meaning the rectangle could be right of that hole? Wait, the condition for being left of the hole is max J < σ(i). If σ(i') ≤ k, then max J ≤ k may be ≥ σ(i'). So the rectangle may not satisfy max J < σ(i') (i.e., may not be left of that hole). So the rectangle could be right of that hole (i.e., covering columns to the right of the hole). But the rectangle's column interval J includes column k, which is >= σ(i')? Actually if σ(i') ≤ k, then column k is to the right of that hole (since hole column ≤ k). So the rectangle would contain the hole at column σ(i')? Not necessarily, because the rectangle's column interval includes column k, but does it include column σ(i')? Since J is contiguous from some leftmost column L to rightmost column R = max J ≤ k. If σ(i') ≤ k, then the interval [L,R] may include σ(i') if L ≤ σ(i') ≤ R. The rectangle's row interval includes row i', so if σ(i') is within J, then the tile would cover the hole at (i', σ(i')). Since holes cannot be covered, the rectangle must avoid covering that hole. So to avoid covering the hole in row i', the interval J must not contain σ(i'). Since J includes k, and σ(i') ≤ k, the only way to avoid covering the hole is to have J entirely to the right of σ(i') (i.e., L > σ(i')). But J includes k, which is ≥ σ(i'), so L could be > σ(i') if L > σ(i') ≤ k. For example, suppose σ(i') = 2, k = 5, J = [4,5] (L=4,R=5). Then J does not contain 2, so the hole is not covered. So the rectangle is to the right of the hole in that row. So indeed a rectangle covering a left cell could be right of holes in other rows. So the claim \"any tile that covers a cell of L must be a left-rectangle\" is not necessarily true: a tile covering a left cell could be a rectangle that is left of the hole in that row but right of holes in other rows; however, the definition of left-rectangle requires being left of holes in all rows. So the claim may be false. But the solution uses this claim to argue that left-rectangles covering L cells are disjoint from right-rectangles covering R cells. If the claim is false, the lower bound argument may be flawed.\n\nWe need to examine the solution's definitions and lemmas more carefully. The solution defines L as set of cells (σ^{-1}(k+1), k) for k=1..n-1. These are cells left of the hole in their row (hole at column k+1). Similarly, R are cells right of the hole in their row (hole at column k). Then they prove Lemma 1: Any left rectangle (i.e., rectangle left of holes in all its rows) can contain at most one L cell. Lemma 2: Any right rectangle can contain at most one R cell. Then they claim: \"Any tile that covers a cell of L must be a left-rectangle, because the cell lies left of its hole and a rectangle containing it cannot extend to the right of that hole (otherwise the hole would be inside the rectangle).\" This is the claim we need to examine.\n\nLet's think: Suppose a tile T = I × J contains a cell c_k = (σ^{-1}(k+1), k). The tile's column interval J includes column k. Since the hole for that row is at column k+1, J must not include column k+1. So max J ≤ k. So for that row, the rectangle is left of the hole. However, for other rows i in I, the rectangle may have holes at columns that are less than or equal to max J, causing the rectangle to contain those holes. But the rectangle must avoid all holes. So for each row i in I, we must have that the hole column σ(i) is not in J. Since J is contiguous and includes column k, for any row i where σ(i) ≤ k, we must have that σ(i) is not in J. That can be achieved if J is a subinterval that does not include σ(i). Since J includes k, and σ(i) ≤ k, the only way to avoid including σ(i) is if J's left endpoint L > σ(i). So J must be entirely to the right of σ(i). So for rows where the hole is to the left of column k, the rectangle is to the right of those holes. So the rectangle is not left of all holes; it's left of some holes (including the hole in the row of the L cell) and right of others. So the rectangle is not a left-rectangle as defined (which requires being left of holes in all rows). So the claim is false.\n\nThus the lower bound argument that each left-rectangle can contain at most one L cell and each right-rectangle can contain at most one R cell, and that each tile covering L must be left-rectangle, is flawed. However, maybe the solution's definitions of left-rectangle and right-rectangle are not as strict as we think. Let's read the solution's definitions:\n\nIn Lemma 1, they define left rectangle as: \"Let T=I×J satisfy (1) and suppose that for every i∈I we have max J < σ(i) (i.e. the rectangle lies left of the hole in each of its rows).\" So they require that for all rows i in I, the rectangle lies left of the hole. That's the definition of left rectangle.\n\nThen they claim: \"Any tile that covers a cell of L must be a left‑rectangle, because the cell lies left of its hole and a rectangle containing it cannot extend to the right of that hole (otherwise the hole would be inside the rectangle).\" This is indeed a misinterpretation: The rectangle cannot extend to the right of that hole in that particular row, but it could extend to the right of holes in other rows as long as those holes are not within J. But if the rectangle extends to the right of a hole in another row, it would contain that hole, which is not allowed. So the rectangle cannot extend to the right of any hole in any row it covers. So indeed, for any row i in I, the rectangle must be either left of the hole (max J < σ(i)) or right of the hole (min J > σ(i)). But can it be left for some rows and right for others? Let's examine: Suppose J is an interval [L,R]. For a given row i, if σ(i) < L, then the rectangle is right of the hole (since min J = L > σ(i)). If σ(i) > R, then the rectangle is left of the hole (since max J = R < σ(i)). If σ(i) is between L and R inclusive, then the rectangle would contain the hole, which is not allowed. So for each row i in I, either σ(i) < L (hole left of rectangle) or σ(i) > R (hole right of rectangle). So the rectangle can be left of some holes and right of others, but not both left and right relative to the same hole. So the rectangle can be left of some holes and right of others. So the rectangle is not necessarily uniformly left or uniformly right across all rows. The solution's classification into left-rectangle and right-rectangle is too restrictive: they require uniform leftness or rightness across all rows. But a rectangle could be left of some holes and right of others, as long as it does not contain any hole. The solution's claim that any tile covering an L cell must be a left-rectangle is false, because the tile could be left of the hole in that row but right of holes in other rows (if those holes are left of the rectangle). However, can such a rectangle exist while covering an L cell? Let's examine a concrete example: n=3, holes at (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). L cells: (σ^{-1}(2),1) = (2,1) and (σ^{-1}(3),2) = (3,2). So L = {(2,1), (3,2)}. Consider tile covering (2,1) and (3,2) together? That would be rectangle I = {2,3}, J = {1,2}. This rectangle includes cells (2,1), (2,2), (3,1), (3,2). But (2,2) is a hole, so not allowed. So cannot include both L cells in same rectangle. But consider tile covering (2,1) only, maybe also (3,1) (vertical strip column 1 rows 2-3). That rectangle I = {2,3}, J = {1}. This rectangle's column interval J = {1}. For row 2, hole at column 2 > R=1, so rectangle left of hole (good). For row 3, hole at column 3 > R=1, also left of hole. So this rectangle is left of holes in both rows, thus left-rectangle. So that matches claim.\n\nNow consider a more complex permutation where holes are not monotonic. For example, n=4, holes at positions (1,2), (2,4), (3,1), (4,3). So permutation σ: σ(1)=2, σ(2)=4, σ(3)=1, σ(4)=3. L cells: For k=1..3: c_1 = (σ^{-1}(2),1) = (1,1) because σ(1)=2. c_2 = (σ^{-1}(3),2) = (4,2) because σ(4)=3. c_3 = (σ^{-1}(4),3) = (2,3) because σ(2)=4. So L = {(1,1), (4,2), (2,3)}. Let's examine tile covering (1,1) and (2,3) maybe? That would be rectangle I = {1,2}, J = {1,3}? But J must be contiguous: {1,2,3} includes column 2. That rectangle includes cells (1,2) which is a hole (since (1,2) is a hole). So not allowed. How about tile covering (1,1) and (4,2)? That rectangle I = {1,4}, J = {1,2} includes rows 1-4 and columns 1-2. That includes holes (1,2) and (3,1) maybe? Actually (3,1) is a hole? Yes, (3,1) is a hole. So not allowed. So maybe any tile covering an L cell must be left-