, the solution also says \"any tile that covers a cell of L must be a left-rectangle\". That is false. So the lower bound argument is flawed.\n\nBut perhaps the construction they propose (vertical strips) actually uses only left-rectangles and right-rectangles? Actually, the construction uses vertical strips that are either above or below the hole in each column. Those strips are either left- or right-? Let's examine: For each column j, they place a rectangle covering rows 1 to r_j-1 (above the hole) and rows r_j+1 to n (below). These are vertical strips that are contiguous intervals of rows, but column interval is just {j}. So these are vertical strips of width 1. For each column j, the vertical strip above the hole is left of the hole? The hole is at row r_j, column j. The rectangle is at column j, rows less than r_j. The hole is at same column, but rectangle does not include the hole's row. So does the rectangle lie left of the hole in its row? The concept of left/right is defined relative to columns, not rows. So left/right classification is based on column intervals relative to hole columns. For a rectangle of width 1 column j, the rectangle's column interval J = {j}. For any row i, the hole column sigma(i) may be less than j, equal to j, or greater than j. If sigma(i) = j, then the hole is in column j, but the rectangle includes column j, so if the rectangle includes row i, it would intersect the hole. However, the rectangle's row interval includes only rows that are not the hole row for column j. So for any row i in I, sigma(i) cannot be j (since that row is the hole row). So for any row i in I, sigma(i) ≠ j. So either sigma(i) < j (hole left of rectangle) or sigma(i) > j (hole right of rectangle). So each vertical strip is either left-rectangle (if sigma(i) > j for all i in I) or right-rectangle (if sigma(i) < j for all i in I). Let's examine: For the vertical strip above the hole in column j, the rows are 1,...,r_j-1. For any such row i, sigma(i) is the col