j\\le n\\).  \nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \nHence the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation\n\\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to the diagonal case  \n\nPermuting rows by the permutation \\(\\tau(i)=i\\) and columns by \\(\\tau' (j)=\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\) merely relabels the coordinates.  \nSince the problem is invariant under simultaneous row and column permutations, we may assume\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=i\\qquad\\text{for all }i,\n\\]\ni.e. the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Geometry of the remaining cells  \n\nAfter the above relabeling the uncovered cells are exactly the diagonal.  \nConsequently the set of covered cells is\n\\[\nC=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\neq j\\}=U^{c}.\n\\]\nThe set \\(C\\) splits into two disjoint parts:\n\\[\nC^{+}= \\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}\\qquad\\text{(strictly upper‑triangular)},\n\\]\n\\[\nC^{-}= \\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}\\qquad\\text{(strictly lower‑triangular)}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Any rectangle stays in one region  \n\nA rectangle on the grid is determined by two intervals of rows and columns:\n\\[\nR=[a,b],\\qquad S=[c,d]\\qquad (1\\le a\\le b\\le n,\\;1\\le c\\le d\\le n).\n\\]\nThe rectangle consists of all cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\in R,\\;j\\in S\\).  \nIf \\(R\\) and \\(S\\) intersect, i.e. there exists an index \\(k\\) with \\(a\\le k\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le k\\le d\\), then the cell \\((k,k)\\) belongs to the rectangle; this cell is uncovered, contradicting the requirement that a tile may cover only covered cells.  \nHence for any admissible rectangle we must have either \\(b<c\\) (the whole rectangle lies above the diagonal) or \\(d<a\\) (the whole rectangle lies below the diagonal).  \nThus every tile is contained entirely in \\(C^{+}\\) or entirely in \\(C^{-}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Lower bound for the upper‑triangular region  \n\nConsider the cells\n\\[\n\\mathcal{K}^{+}=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\nThese are the leftmost cells of each row of \\(C^{+}\\); we call them **upper corners**.  \n\nLet \\(R=[a,b]\\) and \\(S=[c,d]\\) be the row and column intervals of a rectangle lying in \\(C^{+}\\).  \nSince the rectangle is above the diagonal we have \\(b<c\\).  \nIf the rectangle contains two distinct upper corners \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\), then\n\\(i\\in R\\) and \\(i+1\\in S\\), as well as \\(j\\in R\\) and \\(j+1\\in S\\).  \nBecause \\(i<j\\), we obtain \\(j\\ge i+1\\).  \nBut \\(b<c\\) implies \\(\\max R < \\min S\\), i.e. \\(j\\le b < c\\le i+1\\), a contradiction.  \nHence **any rectangle contained in \\(C^{+}\\) can contain at most one upper corner**.\n\nSince the \\(n-1\\) upper corners are pairwise distinct, covering all of \\(C^{+}\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Lower bound for the lower‑triangular region  \n\nThe same argument applied to the set of **lower corners**\n\\[\n\\mathcal{K}^{-}=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\nshows that any rectangle lying in \\(C^{-}\\) contains at most one lower corner.  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(C^{-}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Global lower bound  \n\nAll tiles lie either in \\(C^{+}\\) or in \\(C^{-}\\); the two families are disjoint.  \nTherefore any tiling of the whole board must use at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7. Construction attaining the bound  \n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n*Upper‑triangular tiles.*  \nFor each row index \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place a horizontal tile\n\\[\nT^{+}_{i}= \\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(T^{+}_{i}\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle; its left side lies on the grid line \\(x=i\\) and its right side on \\(x=n\\).\n\n*Lower‑triangular tiles.*  \nFor each column index \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place a vertical tile\n\\[\nT^{-}_{j}= \\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(T^{-}_{j}\\) is an \\((n-j)\\times 1\\) rectangle; its top side lies on the grid line \\(y=j\\) and its bottom side on \\(y=n\\).\n\n**Verification.**  \n\n- Every tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines.  \n- The tiles are pairwise disjoint: a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) belongs only to \\(T^{+}_{i}\\); a cell with \\(i>j\\) belongs only to \\(T^{-}_{j}\\).  \n- The union of all tiles is exactly \\(C^{+}\\cup C^{-}= \\{(i,j)\\mid i\\neq j\\}\\); the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered.  \n- In each row \\(i\\) the uncovered cell is \\((i,i)\\); in each column \\(j\\) the uncovered cell is \\((j,j)\\). Hence the required condition is satisfied.\n\nThe number of tiles used is\n\\[\n| \\{T^{+}_{i}\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}| + |\\{T^{-}_{j}\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}| = (n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8. Conclusion for \\(n=2025\\)\n\nWith \\(n=2025\\) we obtain the optimal number\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2\\cdot 2024 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nThus the minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
