                "content": ".**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have found a complete solution. The minimum number of tiles required is  \n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares.**  \n   Since each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares forms a permutation matrix: there is a permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (with \\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered squares are precisely \\((i,p(i))\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n2. **Lower bound – left side.**  \n   For every row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) the cell \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) is the rightmost covered cell in that row’s left part. Any tile covering this cell cannot extend to the right of column \\(p(i)-1\\); hence its rightmost column is exactly \\(p(i)-1\\).  \n   Because the values \\(p(i)-1\\) are all distinct for different rows, at least one distinct tile is needed for each of the \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(p(i)>1\\). Thus at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover all left‑hand cells.\n\n3. **Lower bound – right side.**  \n   Dually, for every row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)<n\\) the cell \\((i,p(i)+1)\\) is the leftmost covered cell in that row’s right part. Any tile covering it cannot extend left of column \\(p(i)+1\\); thus its leftmost column is exactly \\(p(i)+1\\).  \n   The values \\(p(i)+1\\) are distinct for the \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(p(i)<n\\), so at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed for the right‑hand cells.\n\n4. **Combined lower bound.**  \n   A tile cannot contain cells from both sides of a hole in the same row, so the tiles covering the left side and those covering the right side are disjoint. Consequently any tiling uses at least  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n   tiles.\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \n   - For each row \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a horizontal tile covering \\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,i-1)\\) – the whole left part of the row.  \n   - For each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a horizontal tile covering \\((i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\) – the whole right part of the row.  \n\n   These are exactly \\(n-1\\) left‑side tiles and \\(n-1\\) right‑side tiles, all disjoint, covering every square except the diagonal holes. Hence the bound \\(2n-2\\) is achievable.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) the minimum number of tiles is \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA tiling must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nHence the uncovered squares are a set  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nAll other unit squares are to be covered by axis‑parallel rectangles (tiles) whose interiors contain no point of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for the left side\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) define its **left interval**\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<p(i)\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(p(i)=1\\) then \\(L_i\\) is empty; otherwise \\(L_i\\) consists of the cells\n\\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,p(i)-1)\\).\n\nConsider a row with \\(p(i)>1\\).  \nThe cell \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) is the rightmost cell of \\(L_i\\).  \nAny tile that contains this cell cannot contain column \\(p(i)\\), because \\((i,p(i))\\) is uncovered.  \nConsequently the tile’s rightmost column is exactly \\(p(i)-1\\).\n\nSince the numbers \\(p(i)-1\\) are distinct for distinct rows (the map \\(i\\mapsto p(i)\\) is a permutation), the rightmost column of a tile covering \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) is different for each such row.  \nThus each of the \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(p(i)>1\\) forces a distinct tile.  \nHence any covering of the set \\(\\bigcup_i L_i\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the right side\n\nDefine the **right interval** of row \\(i\\) by  \n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(p(i)=n\\) then \\(R_i\\) is empty; otherwise it consists of the cells\n\\((i,p(i)+1),\\dots ,(i,n)\\).\n\nFor a row with \\(p(i)<n\\) the cell \\((i,p(i)+1)\\) is the leftmost cell of \\(R_i\\).  \nAny tile containing this cell cannot contain column \\(p(i)\\); therefore its leftmost column is exactly \\(p(i)+1\\).\n\nThe values \\(p(i)+1\\) are distinct for the \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(p(i)<n\\).  \nHence each of those rows forces a distinct tile covering its right interval.  \nConsequently any covering of \\(\\bigcup_i R_i\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Combined lower bound\n\nA tile cannot contain cells from both \\(L_i\\) and \\(R_i\\) of the same row, because the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\) separates the two intervals.  \nTherefore the tiles covering the left side and those covering the right side are disjoint families.  \n\nFrom Sections 1 and 2 we obtain  \n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \n\n*For the left side.*  \nFor each row \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a horizontal tile covering the cells\n\\[\n(i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,i-1).\n\\]\nThese are \\(n-1\\) tiles; they cover all cells left of the diagonal.\n\n*For the right side.*  \nFor each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a horizontal tile covering the cells\n\\[\n(i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n).\n\\]\nThese are another \\(n-1\\) tiles; they cover all cells right of the diagonal.\n\nAll tiles are axis‑parallel, their interiors are disjoint, and together they cover every unit square except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), one per row and one per column.  \nThe total number of tiles is\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is attainable; therefore it is the minimum possible number of tiles.  \nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]"
