,(i,i-1)\\) – the whole left part of the row.  \n   - For each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a horizontal tile covering \\((i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\) – the whole right part of the row.  \n\n   These are exactly \\(n-1\\) left‑side tiles and \\(n-1\\) right‑side tiles, all disjoint, covering every square except the diagonal holes. Hence the bound \\(2n-2\\) is achievable.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) the minimum number of tiles is \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA tiling must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nHence the uncovered squares are a set  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nAll other unit squares are to be covered by axis‑parallel rectangles (tiles) whose interiors contain no point of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for the left side\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) define its **left interval**\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<p(i)\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(p(i)=1\\) then \\(L_i\\) is empty; otherwise \\(L_i\\) consists of the cells\n\\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,p(i)-1)\\).\n\nConsider a row with \\(p(i)>1\\).  \nThe cell \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) is the rightmost cell of \\(L_i\\).  \nAny tile that contains this cell cannot contain column \\(p(i)\\), because \\((i,p(i))\\) is uncovered.  \nConsequently the tile’s rightmost column is exactly \\(p(i)-1\\).\n\nSince the numbers \\(p(i)-1\\) are distinct for distinct rows (the map \\(i\\mapsto p(i)\\) is a permutation), the rightmost column of a tile covering \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) is different for each such row.  \nThus each of the \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(p(i)>1\\) forces a distinct tile.  \nHence any covering of the set \\(\\bigcup_i L_i\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the right side\n