\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{run}_1(j)+\\operatorname{run}_0(j)\n=1+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\mathbf 1\\{a_i(j)\\neq a_{i+1}(j)\\},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathbf 1\\{\\cdot\\}\\) denotes the indicator function.  \nInsert (6) into (5) and interchange the order of summation:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\#\\text{tiles}\n&\\ge\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\\Bigl(1+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\mathbf 1\\{a_i(j)\\neq a_{i+1}(j)\\}\\Bigr)\\\\\n&=(n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\\mathbf 1\\{a_i(j)\\neq a_{i+1}(j)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFix \\(i\\) and examine the inner sum.  \nAssume without loss of generality that \\(p(i)<p(i+1)\\).  \nThen for a column index \\(j\\)\n\n\\[\na_i(j)\\neq a_{i+1}(j)\n\\iff p(i)\\le j<p(i+1).\n\\]\n\nHence exactly the integers \\(j=p(i),p(i)+1,\\dots ,p(i+1)-1\\) satisfy the inequality, and therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\\mathbf 1\\{a_i(j)\\neq a_{i+1}(j)\\}=p(i+1)-p(i)=|p(i+1)-p(i)|.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe same equality holds when \\(p(i)>p(i+1)\\).  \nSubstituting (8) into (7) yields the universal lower bound  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|p(i)-p(i+1)|}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, the terms \\(|p(i)-p(i+1)|\\) are positive integers, each at least \\(1\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|p(i)-p(i+1)|\\ge n-1,\n\\]\n\nand (9) gives the simpler bound  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus **any** tiling must contain at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A tiling attaining the bound\n\nTake the identity permutation  \n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\qquad(1\\le i\\le n).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place the two vertical tiles\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT_j^{\\text{below}}&=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\nT_j^{\\text{above}}&=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBoth are rectangles (a single column together with a contiguous block of rows).  \nColumn \\(n\\) needs no tile because there is no cell to its right, and column \\(1\\) needs no “above’’ tile because there is no row above it.\n\n**Verification.**  \nConsider any non‑diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\).\n\n*If \\(j<i\\):* then \\(j\\) lies left of the hole \\((i,i)\\); the cell belongs to \\(T_j^{\\text{below}}\\) because \\(i\\ge j+1\\).  \n*If \\(j>i\\):* then \\(j\\) lies right of the hole; the cell belongs to \\(T_j^{\\text{above}}\\) because \\(i\\le j-1\\).\n\nThus every non‑diagonal cell is covered exactly once.  \nThe diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) are not contained in any tile (column \\(i\\) has no tile covering row \\(i\\)), so each row and each column contains precisely one uncovered unit square, as required.\n\nThe construction uses two tiles for each of the \\(n-1\\) columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\); hence it employs exactly  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nrectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Minimality\n\nEquation (10) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, while the construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]"
