--------------------------\n#### 5.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\nTake the diagonal configuration (3).  \nFor every column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place the two vertical rectangles\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_j^{\\text{above}}&=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\\\\[2pt]\nR_j^{\\text{below}}&=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBoth are products of an interval of rows with the single column \\(\\{j\\}\\); therefore they are legitimate tiles.  \nIf \\(j=1\\) the \u201cabove\u2019\u2019 rectangle is empty and may be omitted; if \\(j=n-1\\) the \u201cbelow\u2019\u2019 rectangle has height \\(1\\) and is still allowed.\n\n**Verification.**  \nLet \\((i,j)\\) be any off\u2011diagonal cell, i.e. \\(i\\neq j\\).\n\n*If \\(i<j\\):* then \\(j\\ge 2\\) and \\(i\\le j-1\\); consequently \\((i,j)\\in R_j^{\\text{above}}\\).\n\n*If \\(i>j\\):* then \\(j\\le n-1\\) and \\(i\\ge j+1\\); consequently \\((i,j)\\in R_j^{\\text{below}}\\).\n\nThus every cell of \\(C\\) belongs to exactly one of the rectangles (8).  \nThe diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) are not contained in any rectangle, so each row and each column contains precisely one uncovered square, as required.\n\nThe total number of rectangles used is\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{above rectangles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{below rectangles}}\n=2(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nInequality (7) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, while construction (8)\u2011(9) exhibits a tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete board of size \\(2025\\times2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]"
