≤2000 tokens, but JSON is short.\n\nNow, we need to evaluate the solution.\n\nFirst, we need to understand the problem: We have a 2025x2025 grid of unit squares. We want to place rectangular tiles (axis-aligned, sides on grid lines) covering some squares, each unit square covered at most once. Each row and each column must have exactly one unit square not covered (i.e., a hole). Determine the minimum number of tiles needed.\n\nThe solution approach: Represent uncovered squares as a permutation p(i) for each row i, unique column per row, and each column also has exactly one uncovered square, so p is a permutation. Then they permute columns to align holes on diagonal (by applying column permutation tau = p^{-1}). This is valid because we can permute columns (relabel columns) without affecting the tile covering property? Actually, we must check if column permutation is allowed: The problem is about a fixed board; we cannot permute columns. However, they argue that we can apply a column permutation to the board, which is a symmetry of the problem: If we have a tiling with holes at positions (i, p(i)), we can rename columns via a permutation to get a new tiling where holes are at (i, i). But does this preserve the number of tiles? Yes, because we are just renaming columns; the shape of tiles is preserved as rectangles (since columns are permuted globally). However, the transformation is not a physical transformation of the board; it's a relabeling. Since the problem asks for the minimum number of tiles, we can consider any arrangement; we can apply a column permutation to any arrangement to get a new arrangement with same number of tiles. So we can assume w.l.o.g. that holes are on diagonal. That seems valid.\n\nThen they define critical cells: left critical cells L_i = (i, i-1) for i≥2, and upper critical cells U_j = (j-1, j) for j≥2. They claim there are 2n-2 such cells, all covered. Then they prove a lemma: any rectangle can contain at most one critical cell. The proof is by contradiction: if a rectangle contains two left critical cells, then it must also contain a diagonal hole, which is not allowed. Similarly for two upper critical cells. For one left and one upper, they argue that rectangle must contain a diagonal hole as well. This seems plausible.\n\nThus each rectangle can contain at most one critical cell, so need at least 2n-2 rectangles. Then they construct a tiling with exactly 2n-2 rectangles: For each column j (1 to n-1), place two vertical rectangles: one covering rows 1..j-1 in column j (above), and one covering rows j+1..n in column j (below). This covers all off-diagonal cells: if i<j, cell (i,j) is in above rectangle; if i>j, cell (i,j) is in below rectangle. The diagonal cells (i,i) are uncovered. This uses 2(n-1) rectangles, i.e., 2n-2. So the lower bound matches the construction, thus minimal number is 2n-2 = 4048.\n\nThus answer appears correct.\n\nNow we need to check reasoning validity: The methods used include: reduction via column permutation (symmetry), definition of critical cells, lemma using rectangle containment arguments, counting lower bound, explicit construction using vertical rectangles. All are valid mathematical methods: case analysis, counting, constructive proof, contradiction. So reasoning is valid.\n\nNow check presentation: Are there any critical errors? Possibly the claim that column permutation does not change the nature of the problem: we need to ensure that after permuting columns, the rectangles remain rectangles. Since the permutation is a global reordering of columns, each rectangle's columns become a set of columns that may not be contiguous after permutation. Wait: The rectangle is defined as a product of intervals of rows and columns (consecutive integer intervals). If we permute columns arbitrarily, the image of a rectangle may not be a rectangle (i.e., columns may not be contiguous). However, they claim that permuting columns as a whole (i.e., moving entire columns) preserves