rder columns, the shape of a rectangle (which is a contiguous block of columns) will remain a rectangle because the columns are moved as whole blocks, preserving adjacency between columns that were originally adjacent? Actually, moving columns individually can change adjacency: If we reorder columns arbitrarily, columns that were originally adjacent may become separated. But the rectangle's columns are a contiguous interval; after moving each column to a new position, the set of positions occupied by those columns may not be contiguous. However, if we physically reorder columns as a whole, we are rearranging the order of columns. The rectangle originally spanned columns a..b. After reordering, the columns a..b are now placed at positions tau(a), tau(a+1),..., tau(b). If tau is not monotonic, these positions may not be consecutive. So the rectangle may become a \"non-rectangular\" shape (a set of columns not contiguous). So the claim that \"whole columns are moved together, therefore every rectangle remains a rectangle\" is false in general. However, they might be using a different notion: they consider the permutation as a relabeling of columns, not a physical movement. In that case, the rectangle's definition is based on original column indices; after relabeling, the rectangle is still defined by the same set of squares, but the indices change. The rectangle property (i.e., being a product of intervals of rows and columns) is not invariant under arbitrary relabeling of columns. The property \"sides lie on grid lines\" is invariant under column permutation because the grid lines are also permuted? Actually, if we permute columns, the grid lines are also permuted accordingly; the shape of a rectangle in the new labeling may not be axis-aligned. But the solution's argument is typical: Since we can permute columns, we can assume the holes are on the diagonal. This is a known technique: we can reorder columns to align holes on diagonal because we can treat the permutati