der: i.e., we reorder the axes so that the new column index is the position in the permutation order. But we cannot guarantee that J' is consecutive in that order either. Actually, we can define a new labeling of columns such that the order of columns is given by the permutation tau. That is, we reorder the columns physically, not just rename them. If we physically move columns to new positions, then the rectangle shape may be broken. So the claim is questionable.\n\nNevertheless, many combinatorial problems use the \"without loss of generality\" argument that we can assume the holes are on the diagonal by permuting columns. This is often valid because the property of being covered by rectangles is invariant under column permutations: if you have a tiling of the board, you can permute columns and get another tiling (maybe not axis-aligned?). Actually, the property \"each tile is a rectangle with sides on grid lines\" is defined relative to the grid lines. If we permute columns, the grid lines are also permuted, but the shape of each tile may become a shape that is not a rectangle relative to the new grid lines. However, the new grid lines are still vertical lines at integer x-coordinates after permutation? If we reorder columns, the vertical lines are no longer equally spaced? Actually, we can think of the grid as a set of vertical lines at integer x positions 0,1,...,n. Permuting columns means we are swapping the columns, which are the unit squares between vertical lines x=j-1 and x=j. If we swap columns, the vertical lines remain at the same positions; we are moving the squares between them. The shape of a rectangle originally spanning columns a..b is a region between vertical lines x=a-1 and x=b. After swapping columns, the squares originally in that region are moved to other positions, but the rectangle region (the region between x=a-1 and x=b) is now filled with squares that were originally elsewhere. So the rectangle region is still a rectangle (the region between those vertical lines). However, the covering set of squares may be different: the rectangle region now contains squares that were originally part of other columns. But the tile is defined as a set of unit squares; after swapping columns, the tile's shape (the region) remains a rectangle because we moved the squares, not the region. Wait, we need to be precise: The tile is a set of unit squares that we place on the board. If we permute columns, we are permuting the positions of the unit squares, not the tile shape. The tile shape is defined by the set of squares it occupies. If we physically move columns, we move the squares, but the tile shape will be the same shape relative to the board because we moved the squares, not the tile. Actually, think of a board as a fixed grid of positions; we place tiles covering certain positions. If we permute columns, we are reassigning which column index each position belongs to. But the tile covers a set of positions; after relabeling columns, the tile still covers the same set of positions (just with different column indices). The shape of the tile relative to the board is unchanged; it's still a rectangle because the positions it covers are still contiguous in the original geometry. The column indices are just labels; the geometry is unchanged. So the tile remains a rectangle. So the argument that column permutation preserves rectangle property is valid if we treat permutation as a relabeling of columns, not a physical rearrangement. In that case, the rectangle property (sides on grid lines) is unchanged because the grid lines are unchanged; only the labeling of columns changes. So the rectangle remains a rectangle. So the argument is valid.\n\nThus the reduction to diagonal is valid.\n\nNow, the lemma about critical cells: The proof seems correct. However, we need to check if any hidden assumptions or errors exist. The lemma states that a rectangle can contain at most one critical cell. The proof uses the fact that if a rectangle contains two left critical cells L_i and L_i', then the rectangle's column interval includes i-1 and i'-1, so includes i (since i-1 < i <= i'-1). But does the rectangle's column interval necessarily include i? The column interval J includes columns i-1 and i'-1. Since J is an interval of consecutive integers, it includes all integers between i-1 and i'-1 inclusive. Since i is between i-1 and i'-1 (i-1 < i <= i'-1), i is indeed in J. Similarly, the row interval I includes rows i and i' (since L_i = (i,i-1) means row i is in I, L_i' = (i',i'-1) means row i' is in I). So I includes i and i'. So the rectangle contains (i,i), which is a diagonal hole, contradiction. This is correct.\n\nSimilarly for two upper critical cells.\n\nFor one left and one upper, they consider two cases: i <= j-1 and i > j-1. They argue that in either case rectangle contains (i,i) or (j,j). The reasoning appears correct.\n\nThus lemma is valid.\n\nThus lower bound of 2n-2 is valid.\n\nNow the construction: For each column j (1 to n-1), place two vertical rectangles: above and below the diagonal cell (j,j). They claim that above rectangle includes rows 1..j-1 in column j, below includes rows j+1..n in column j. This covers all off-diagonal cells: For any off-diagonal cell (i,j) with i<j, it's in above rectangle; if i>j, it's in below rectangle. This is correct. For j=1, above rectangle is empty; they can omit it. For j=n-1, below rectangle has height 1 (row n). So total rectangles = 2(n-1). This matches lower bound.\n\nThus the solution is correct and reasoning is valid.\n\nNow check for presentation issues: Are there any critical errors? The solution seems rigorous. However, we need to see if any step might be considered a \"justification gap\". For example, the claim \"Permuting the columns of the board does not change the nature of the problem: whole columns are moved together, therefore every rectangle remains a rectangle\" might be considered a bit informal but it's a valid argument.