\) be the value attached to \\(v\\) and let \\(p(v)=a_{\\text{parent}(v)}\\) (with \\(p(\\text{root})=0\\)).  \n   Define the rectangle  \n\n   \\[\n   R(v)=I(v)\\times\\{p(v)+1,\\dots ,a_v\\}\\qquad(v\\neq\\text{root}).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Key Lemma 2.* For every cell \\((i,j)\\in L\\) there exists a **unique** node \\(v\\) on the path from the leaf representing row \\(i\\) to the root such that  \n\n   \\[\n   p(v)<j\\le a_v .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   (Existence follows because the values \\(a\\) strictly decrease along that path; uniqueness follows from the strict monotonicity.)  \n   Consequently \\((i,j)\\in R(v)\\).  \n   The rectangles \\(\\{R(v):v\\neq\\text{root}\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint (different rows for incomparable nodes, disjoint column intervals for ancestor\u2013descendant pairs) and together partition \\(L\\).  \n   The Cartesian tree has exactly \\(n\\) nodes, the root contributes no rectangle, so we obtain precisely \\(n-1\\) rectangles covering \\(L\\).\n\n5. **Right side.**  \n   Reflect the board horizontally (replace column \\(j\\) by \\(n+1-j\\)).  \n   Under this reflection the uncovered permutation becomes \\(\\sigma'(i)=n+1-\\sigma(i)\\) and the numbers \\(b_i=n-\\sigma(i)\\) form a permutation of \\(\\{0,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \n   Applying the same Cartesian\u2011tree construction to \\((b_1,\\dots ,b_n)\\) yields a partition of \\(R\\) into another \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n6. **Optimality.**  \n   The lower bound (1) and the explicit tiling with exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles coincide; therefore the minimal possible number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{4048}.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nSince each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, there exists a permutation  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\d